      <section class="tg-section" id="holiness">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Root Question — What Kind of Thing Is Holiness?</h2>

        <p>Korah's premise is not heresy. It is
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 19:6">Exodus 19:6</a> — "you shall be to me a
        kingdom of priests and a holy nation." He is quoting the covenant charter.</p>

        <p>The priestly theology of Leviticus–Numbers answers with a different grammar.
        Holiness there is not a <strong>possession</strong> distributed equally but a
        <strong>gradient</strong> organised by proximity. Milgrom mapped it as concentric
        zones: the camp, the court, the Holy Place, the Holy of Holies; and
        correspondingly the people, the Levites, the priests, the high priest; and
        correspondingly the days, the festivals, the Sabbaths, the Day of Atonement. Mary
        Douglas read the same structure spatially, arguing that Numbers encodes the
        tabernacle's geometry into its narrative shape.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Where the Syllogism Fails</span>
          <p><strong>Korah's argument:</strong> all Israel is holy → therefore no
          Israelite may be excluded from the holy → therefore the Aaronide restriction is
          human self-exaltation.</p>
          <p><strong>The priestly answer:</strong> all Israel is holy <em>as a nation set
          apart</em> → holiness of that kind is a vocation of separation from the nations,
          not a warrant of access to the sanctuary → therefore the restriction is not
          exaltation but ordering, and it is God's ordering, not Moses'.</p>
        </div>

        <p>Two different senses of <em>qadosh</em> are in play. Both are biblical. The
        dispute is not orthodoxy versus heresy but <strong>equivocation</strong> — and
        this is why the passage has been so endlessly usable, because in almost every
        later application the same equivocation is available to both sides.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Canonical Answer</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The canonical answer to Korah is neither Korah's
          nor Aaron's.</p>
          <p>Hebrews does not say that the Aaronide restriction was never legitimate
          (Korah's claim), nor that it stands forever (the priestly claim as Korah heard
          it). It says the restriction was <em>provisional</em>, and has been ended — not
          by democratic assertion but by a superior priesthood.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="christology">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Christology — The Anti-Korah</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Grasping and the Not-Grasping</h3>

        <p>The sharpest typological contrast in Scripture to Korah is not another rebel.
        It is Christ.</p>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead><tr><th></th><th>Status Held</th><th>Response</th><th>Outcome</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Korah</strong></td><td>Kohathite Levite, entrusted with the most holy things (<a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 4:4-15">Num 4:4–15</a>)</td><td>Judges it insufficient; seizes at the higher office</td><td>Destroyed</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Christ</strong></td><td>"Being in the form of God" — the highest status there is</td><td>"Did not count equality with God a thing to be grasped" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Philippians 2:6">Phil 2:6</a>); empties himself</td><td>Exalted</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Hebrews makes the connection explicit and unmistakable: <strong>"No one takes
        this honour for himself, but only when called by God, just as Aaron was. So also
        Christ did not exalt himself to be made a high priest"</strong>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 5:4-5">Hebrews 5:4–5</a>). The author quotes the
        Aaron principle — the very principle Korah violated — and applies it to Christ,
        who satisfies it perfectly.</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">A Caution on Harpagmon</span>
          The exegesis of <em>harpagmon</em> in Philippians 2:6 is genuinely disputed — a
          thing seized versus a thing to be exploited. The contrast holds on the common
          reading; a reader who takes <em>res rapta</em> will want to phrase it as Christ
          not <em>exploiting</em> what he already held, which still stands opposite Korah's
          reaching for what he did not.
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Melchizedek Move</h3>

        <p><a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 7">Hebrews 7</a> is doing something more
        radical than defending Aaron. It argues that a <em>different</em> priesthood —
        Melchizedekian, not Levitical, "not on the basis of a legal requirement concerning
        bodily descent but by the power of an indestructible life" (7:16) — has superseded
        the Aaronide order entirely. There is "a change in the priesthood… and necessarily
        a change in the law as well" (7:12).</p>

        <p>So the Aaronide monopoly that Numbers 17:5 was written to establish is, in
        Hebrews, over. Not because Korah was right, but because the whole system was a
        shadow (8:5, 10:1).</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Veil</h3>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Since we have confidence to enter the holy places by the blood of Jesus… let us
          draw near.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 10:19-22">Hebrews 10:19–22</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>The verb is the priestly <em>drawing near</em>. The synoptics place the same
        point at the cross: the veil torn from top to bottom
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 27:51">Matthew 27:51</a> and parallels).</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Single Most Important Thing to Say About Numbers 16</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The conclusion Korah wanted is Christian doctrine;
          the argument he used remains false. The mechanism is the whole difference.</p>
          <p>Korah wanted the answer <em>yes</em>. He got the answer <em>no</em>, and died.
          The church receives the answer <em>yes</em> — on entirely different grounds. Not
          "we are already holy," but "we have been made holy by the offering of the body of
          Jesus Christ once for all" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 10:10">Hebrews 10:10</a>).
          This is almost never said in a Christian setting.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Aaron Between the Dead and the Living</h3>

        <p>Numbers 17:13 (16:48 English): Aaron runs into the middle of the assembly with
        a censer of incense, "and he stood between the dead and the living, and the plague
        was stopped."</p>

        <p>This is the passage's densest typological node, and both patristic and Puritan
        expositors worked it hard: the mediator who steps <em>into</em> the zone of
        judgment, bearing the means of atonement, and whose interposition halts wrath. The
        instrument is a <strong>censer</strong> — and incense is prayer
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 141:2">Psalm 141:2</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 5:8">Revelation 5:8</a>; 8:3–4). The
        intercessor's prayer is what stands in the breach.</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">The Dark Inversion</span>
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 8:5">Revelation 8:5</a> — the angel fills
          the censer with fire from the altar and hurls it to the earth. The same
          instrument, the same fire, judgment rather than mercy — as in Numbers 16:35,
          where fire from the LORD came out and consumed the incense-bearers.
          <strong>The censer cuts both ways depending on who holds it and by what
          right.</strong>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="office">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Ecclesiology I — Priesthood, Office, and Call</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Dividing Formula</h3>

        <p>The Catholic position, formalised at Vatican II: the common priesthood of the
        faithful and the ministerial priesthood <strong>"differ from one another in
        essence and not only in degree"</strong> (<em>Lumen Gentium</em> 10,
        <em>essentia et non gradu tantum</em>). Trent had already anathematised the
        alternative: Session 23 (1563), canon 7 — those who have not been rightly ordained
        and sent by ecclesiastical and canonical authority "are not lawful ministers of the
        word and of the sacraments." Read against Numbers 16, that is a precise
        formalisation of the Aaronide charter.</p>

        <p>The Protestant position, in Luther's form, admits the second clause and denies
        the first: <strong>office and priesthood are different categories, not different
        grades of the same thing.</strong> All believers are priests
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 2:5">1 Peter 2:5</a>, 9;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 1:6">Revelation 1:6</a>; 5:10). Not all hold
        the <em>ministerium</em>, which requires a call. On this account Korah's error was
        not denying a distinction of essence but seizing an office he had not been given —
        a violation of order, not of ontology.</p>

        <p>Anglican Article XXIII states the shared floor: <em>"It is not lawful for any
        man to take upon him the office of publick preaching, or ministering the Sacraments
        in the Congregation, before he be lawfully called."</em> That sentence is Hebrews
        5:4, which is Numbers 16.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Agreement and Division</span>
          <p><strong>What every party actually agrees on:</strong> ministry is
          <em>conferred</em>, not <em>seized</em>. This is the genuine, uncontested yield
          of Numbers 16 for ecclesiology, and it is held by Rome, Constantinople,
          Canterbury, Geneva, Wittenberg, and most of the free churches.</p>
          <p><strong>What they divide over:</strong> whether the office conferred is
          sacerdotal in kind, who has authority to confer it, and what happens when the
          conferring body is itself judged corrupt.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Internal and External Call</h3>

        <div class="tg-definition">
          <span class="tg-definition__term">Vocatio interna / vocatio externa</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__def">The Reformed distinction between the inward
          persuasion of the Spirit and the church's outward examination and setting apart.
          The inward call does not by itself constitute a call. Turretin, Owen, and the
          Westminster tradition all insist on both — precisely to avoid the Korah charge.</span>
        </div>

        <p>The tradition then divides on what happens when the two conflict:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Quakers</strong> dispense with the external call entirely — the
          Spirit's immediacy is sufficient, and an ordained caste is the return of the
          shadow.</li>
          <li><strong>Wesley</strong> ordained Coke for America on an argument from
          <em>necessity</em> and from his own reading that presbyters and bishops share one
          order (leaning on Jerome, <em>Ep.</em> 146). Charles Wesley's protest was made in
          exactly Korah's key.</li>
          <li><strong>Montanism</strong> is the ancient version of the same fault line, and
          the church's response to it — subordinating charismatic authority to episcopal
          order — set a pattern that Sohm and Harnack fought over in the nineteenth century
          (is church <em>law</em> a betrayal of the church's essence, or its necessary
          form?), that Weber theorised as the routinisation of charisma, and that Käsemann
          revived as the charge of "early catholicism" in the later New Testament.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>None of these debates is settled by Numbers 16, but all of them are conducted
        in its vocabulary.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Apostolic Succession</h3>

        <p><em>1 Clement</em> 43–44 is the seed. Clement takes <strong>Aaron's budding
        rod</strong> as the paradigm of divine certification of ministry: God settles
        disputes about who serves by an unmistakable sign, and then the apostles,
        foreseeing strife over the office, appointed successors and provided for orderly
        succession. That argument, made to a congregation that had deposed its presbyters,
        is the earliest extant Christian appeal to succession, and it is built on
        Numbers 17.</p>

        <p>The modern extensions run through <em>Apostolicae Curae</em> (1896), declaring
        Anglican orders null; the archbishops' reply <em>Saepius Officio</em> (1897); and
        the ecumenical attempt in the Lima text <em>Baptism, Eucharist and Ministry</em>
        (1982) to distinguish <strong>episkopé</strong> (oversight, which all churches
        exercise in some form) from <strong>episcopacy</strong> (the historic threefold
        order) — an attempt to find a settlement that does not require calling anyone
        Korah.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="schism">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Ecclesiology II — Schism, Validity, and the Donatist Question</h2>

        <p>Cyprian made Korah the type of the schismatic, and the argument has a hard edge:
        <em>there is one altar and one priesthood; whoever erects a second is Korah, and
        outside the church there is no salvation.</em></p>

        <p>Augustine, facing the Donatists, had to modify this without abandoning it, and
        the modification is one of the most consequential moves in Western theology:
        <strong>the sacrament belongs to Christ, not to the minister.</strong> A
        schismatic's baptism is valid though illicit; the grace conveyed is Christ's, not
        the administrator's. Hence the later scholastic distinction:</p>

        <div class="tg-definition">
          <span class="tg-definition__term">Validity vs. Regularity</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__def"><strong>Validity</strong> — whether the act is
          what it purports to be, effective <em>ex opere operato</em>.
          <strong>Regularity / liceity</strong> — whether it was performed by the right
          person, in the right way, with authorisation.</span>
        </div>

        <p>This distinction dissolves a great deal of confusion around Korah, because most
        Korah-polemic conflates the two. Numbers 16 is a story about
        <strong>regularity</strong>: who is authorised to approach. It is not, on its face,
        a story about whether unauthorised worship "works."</p>

        <p>And then the censers make even that complicated.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="censers">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Sacramental Theology — The Censers</h2>

        <p>Numbers 17:2–3 (16:37–38 English) is astonishing and underused. After the 250
        are consumed, Eleazar is told to gather their censers, <strong>"for they have
        become holy,"</strong> and hammer them into plating for the altar.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Datum</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The men were destroyed. Their instruments were
          sanctified.</p>
          <p>Objects used in illicit, judgment-incurring worship are not destroyed as
          contaminated; they are absorbed into the legitimate cult and made a permanent
          sign.</p>
        </div>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Against a strict contamination model:</strong> the illicit act did
          not void the objects' holiness. They had been offered before the LORD, and that
          contact was real.</li>
          <li><strong>Toward the Augustinian settlement:</strong> what is offered to God is
          God's, irrespective of the offerer's standing. This is at least analogous to the
          logic that a heretic's baptism is Christ's baptism.</li>
          <li><strong>Toward Origen's allegory:</strong> the church takes up what heresy
          leaves behind and fastens it to the altar — the truths a schismatic movement
          carried are not forfeited with the movement.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Whether these inferences are sound is arguable, but the datum is in the text,
        and it sits awkwardly with the harshest uses of the chapter. The 250 were
        annihilated; their censers went on covering the altar as a memorial.
        <strong>Judgment on persons and disposition of their offerings are not the same
        operation.</strong></p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="election">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Election, the Mixed Church, and Perseverance</h2>

        <p><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 2:19">2 Timothy 2:19</a> quotes Numbers 16:5
        — <em>"The Lord knows those who are his"</em> — and pairs it with <em>"Let everyone
        who names the name of the Lord depart from iniquity."</em> The context is the
        "great house" containing vessels of honour and dishonour (2:20).</p>

        <p>This is the New Testament's most theologically loaded use of the passage, and it
        points somewhere quite different from the hierarchical tradition:</p>

        <ul>
          <li>The Korah crisis is read as a crisis of <strong>the mixed body</strong> — a
          visible assembly containing those who are and are not truly God's.</li>
          <li>The resolution is <strong>God's knowledge</strong>, not human sorting. The
          church's task in the meantime is not to identify the reprobate but to depart from
          iniquity.</li>
          <li>This is a seedbed of Augustine's ecclesiology (the <em>civitas
          permixta</em>), of the Reformed visible/invisible distinction, and of every
          subsequent argument against perfectionist and separatist ecclesiologies — which
          is ironic, since the Korah text is elsewhere the standard weapon <em>for</em>
          separation.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Both Moves Are in the Chapter</span>
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 16:26">Numbers 16:26</a> — "Depart, I pray you,
          from the tents of these wicked men" — is the separatist proof-text, cited
          alongside <a class="ref" data-ref="2 Corinthians 6:17">2 Corinthians 6:17</a>.
          Numbers 16:5 as read by 2 Timothy 2:19 is the anti-separatist one. Donatists and
          Catholics, Puritans and conformists, restorationists and denominationalists have
          all fought inside this single chapter.
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Perseverance and the Fate of the Company</h3>

        <ul>
          <li><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Jude 11-13">Jude 11–13</a></strong> places
          Korah with Cain and Balaam among those "twice dead, uprooted" — the language of
          settled reprobation.</li>
          <li><strong><em>m. Sanhedrin</em> 10:3</strong> preserves the Jewish version of
          the argument: R. Akiva denies the company of Korah a share in the world to come;
          R. Eliezer grants it on <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Samuel 2:6">1 Samuel 2:6</a>.
          The Talmudic elaboration has the sons of Korah on a ledge in Gehinnom, reciting.</li>
          <li><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 26:11">Numbers 26:11</a></strong> —
          the sons of Korah did not die — is the canonical counterweight, and it is a fact,
          not a homiletical softening.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>The 250, note, were <em>"chieftains of the congregation, chosen from the
        assembly, men of renown."</em> This is not an apostasy of the marginal.
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 6:4-8">Hebrews 6:4–8</a> and 10:26–31 stand in the
        same territory: those who have come closest are those with most to lose. Whatever
        one's doctrine of perseverance, the sociological observation holds and preaches.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="solidarity">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Corporate Solidarity, Federal Headship, and the Children</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Hard Problem</h3>

        <p>Numbers 16:27, 32–33: the earth swallows Dathan and Abiram "with their wives,
        their sons, and their little ones," and "all the men who belonged to Korah, and all
        their goods." This is a real theodicy problem and should not be smoothed. The
        nearest parallel is <strong>Achan</strong>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Joshua 7:24-25">Joshua 7:24–25</a>), whose sons and
        daughters are destroyed with him. The distant parallel is the flood, and Sodom.</p>

        <p>The Torah itself supplies the counter-testimony:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 24:16">Deuteronomy 24:16</a></strong>
          — "Fathers shall not be put to death because of their children, nor shall children
          be put to death because of their fathers." An explicit judicial prohibition of the
          very thing narrated.</li>
          <li><strong>Numbers 26:11</strong> — the sons of Korah did not die.</li>
          <li><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Ezekiel 18">Ezekiel 18</a></strong> and
          <strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Jeremiah 31:29-30">Jeremiah 31:29–30</a></strong>
          — the retirement of the sour-grapes proverb; the soul that sins shall die.</li>
          <li>Against which stands <strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 20:5">Exodus 20:5</a></strong>
          / 34:7 — visiting iniquity to the third and fourth generation of those who hate him.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Available Accounts</h3>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>Corporate personality.</strong> H. Wheeler Robinson's classic thesis:
          the ancient self was constituted in the group, so the household shared the head's
          legal standing. Heavily qualified since (Rogerson's critique in particular) but
          not without force. On this reading the households were legally <em>parties</em>,
          not bystanders — and 16:26–27 supports it, since they were warned and did not
          move.</li>
          <li><strong>Household as legal unit.</strong> More modest: the Near Eastern
          <em>bêt 'āb</em> was a corporate legal entity, and its property and dependents
          shared its fate in law. Descriptive of the world of the text, not normative for
          ours.</li>
          <li><strong>Narrative, not statute.</strong> Numbers 16 reports a divine act;
          Deuteronomy 24:16 legislates for human courts. The two are not in contradiction
          because they govern different agents. This is the standard harmonisation and it is
          legitimate as far as it goes — though it leaves the theodicy question fully
          intact, merely relocating it.</li>
          <li><strong>Trajectory.</strong> The canon itself moves, and Numbers 26:11 is the
          movement's first step within the very same book. The chapter that narrates
          household destruction also records that a household was spared.</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Text Supplies Its Own Challenge</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Moses' protest in 16:22 — "O God, the God of the
          spirits of all flesh, shall one man sin, and you be angry with all the
          congregation?" — is an argument <em>against</em> indiscriminate corporate
          judgment, made by the protagonist, and it succeeds.</p>
          <p>God distinguishes. The text that most sharply raises the corporate-guilt
          problem also contains its most explicit biblical challenge. Numbers 16 is a
          recurring data point in the Reformed doctrine of federal headship and imputation
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 5:12-21">Romans 5:12–21</a>) and in every
          objection to it; the question is the same — <em>can the standing of one determine
          the standing of many?</em></p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="intercession">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Theology of Intercession</h2>

        <p>Four times in this narrative wrath is met with intercession rather than argument:</p>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead><tr><th>Verse</th><th>Act</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>16:4</td><td>Moses falls on his face at the accusation</td></tr>
            <tr><td>16:22</td><td>Moses and Aaron fall and plead against corporate destruction — and God distinguishes</td></tr>
            <tr><td>16:45 (17:10)</td><td>They fall on their faces again as the plague begins</td></tr>
            <tr><td>16:47–48 (17:12–13)</td><td>Aaron takes the censer and <em>stands between the dead and the living</em></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>This sits inside the wider Pentateuchal theology of intercession: Abraham at
        Sodom (<a class="ref" data-ref="Genesis 18:22-33">Genesis 18:22–33</a>), Moses
        after the golden calf (<a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 32:11-14">Exodus 32:11–14</a>,
        30–32) and after the spies (<a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 14:13-20">Numbers 14:13–20</a>),
        Phinehas making atonement (<a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 25:11-13">Numbers 25:11–13</a>).
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Ezekiel 22:30">Ezekiel 22:30</a> states the principle
        negatively — "I sought for a man among them who should build up the wall and stand
        in the breach before me for the land, but I found none."</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Narrative's Own Model</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The narrative's model for responding to a challenge
          to one's authority is: do not defend it; fall down; plead for the challengers'
          lives.</p>
          <p>This is not a devotional gloss. It is what the two vindicated figures in the
          chapter actually do, four times, and it is the behaviour that the fire and the
          earth vindicate. The Christological line from here is direct:
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 7:25">Hebrews 7:25</a>,
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 8:34">Romans 8:34</a>,
          <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 2:5">1 Timothy 2:5</a>. The censer standing
          between the dead and the living is the type; the ascended Christ is what it
          typifies.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="discernment">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Discernment Problem — Korah or Jeremiah?</h2>

        <p>This is the practical crux, and the tradition has been much better at asserting
        the categories than at supplying tests. The problem: <strong>prophets attack the
        sanctuary establishment too, and they are commended for it.</strong></p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Jeremiah 7">Jeremiah 7</a> and
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Jeremiah 26">26</a></strong> — the temple sermon.
          Jeremiah stands in the temple gate and tells Judah its confidence in the sanctuary
          is a lie. The priests and prophets seize him: "You shall die!" (26:8). He is tried
          for a capital offence.</li>
          <li><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Amos 7:10-17">Amos 7:10–17</a></strong> —
          Amaziah, priest of Bethel, sends word to the king: "Amos has conspired against you
          in the midst of the house of Israel." That is the Korah charge, laid by the
          establishment against a true prophet, in the technical vocabulary of sedition.</li>
          <li><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Micah 3:11">Micah 3:11</a></strong> —
          priests teaching for a price, prophets divining for money, and all of them leaning
          on the LORD.</li>
          <li><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 23">Matthew 23</a></strong> — Jesus
          on those who sit in Moses' seat. Note 23:2–3: the office is honoured (<em>do what
          they tell you</em>) while the occupants are excoriated. The office/occupant
          distinction, made by Jesus.</li>
          <li><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 2:11-14">Galatians 2:11–14</a></strong>
          — Paul opposes Peter to his face, publicly, and records it.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>So the charge "you are Korah" cannot be self-verifying, or Amaziah wins and Amos
        loses.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Criteria the Tradition Has Actually Offered</span>
          <p><strong>1. Motive and manner</strong> — <em>m. Avot</em> 5:17. Is the dispute
          "for the sake of Heaven"? The test is not whether one disagrees but whether the
          disagreement seeks truth or seeks the disputant's own elevation. Korah wanted the
          priesthood; Jeremiah wanted nothing and lost everything.</p>
          <p><strong>2. Conformity to prior revelation</strong> —
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 13:1-5">Deuteronomy 13:1–5</a>. Even a
          sign-working prophet is false if he leads away from the LORD. Content outranks
          power.</p>
          <p><strong>3. What is sought</strong> — the prophets seek <em>reform of the office
          by its own standard</em>; Korah sought <em>possession of the office</em>. This is
          probably the sharpest single test available.</p>
          <p><strong>4. Willingness to suffer rather than seize</strong> — Jeremiah in the
          cistern, Amos expelled, Micaiah on bread and water, Christ before Pilate. Korah
          organised 250 chieftains.</p>
          <p><strong>5. Vindication left to God</strong> — Gamaliel's principle,
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 5:38-39">Acts 5:38–39</a>, explicitly citing failed
          movements that followed the Korah pattern. Slow, unsatisfying, and the most
          honest.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">An Unresolved Tension, Named Rather Than Papered</span>
          There is a real tension between criterion 2 and the mechanism of Numbers 16
          itself, which settles the question by miraculous ordeal — exactly what
          Deuteronomy 13 warns cannot be decisive. The tradition has never fully harmonised
          these.
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Abuse of the Charge</h3>

        <p>The modern fusion of Korah with <em>"touch not the Lord's anointed"</em>
        deserves specific attention because it is a category error twice over.
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 105:15">Psalm 105:15</a>
        (= <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Chronicles 16:22">1 Chronicles 16:22</a>) refers to
        the <strong>patriarchs</strong>, sojourners whom God protected from kings;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Samuel 24">1 Samuel 24</a> and 26 concern
        <strong>David refusing to kill Saul</strong> — that is, refusing regicide, not
        refusing criticism. David does in fact rebuke Saul, loudly, from a hillside,
        immediately afterward (24:9–15). Neither text says anything about questioning a
        religious leader's teaching or conduct.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">A Workable Test for the Charge Itself</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Ask whether the accusation is being made <em>by</em>
          the one whose position is challenged.</p>
          <p>In Numbers 16 the charge of rebellion is not brought by Moses — Moses says "in
          the morning the LORD will show" and falls on his face. The verdict comes from
          outside the dispute. <strong>A leader who names his critics Korah has,
          structurally, taken the role of judge in his own cause, which is the one thing
          Moses conspicuously refuses to do.</strong></p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="resistance">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Political Theology and Resistance</h2>

        <p><strong>For submission:</strong>
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 13:1-7">Romans 13:1–7</a>,
        <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 2:13-17">1 Peter 2:13–17</a>,
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Titus 3:1">Titus 3:1</a> — and Numbers 16 as the narrative
        demonstration that opposing God's appointed order is opposing God.</p>

        <p><strong>For resistance:</strong>
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 5:29">Acts 5:29</a> (we must obey God rather than
        men), the Hebrew midwives (<a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 1">Exodus 1</a>),
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Daniel 3">Daniel 3</a> and 6,
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 13">Revelation 13</a>.</p>

        <p><strong>The mediating tradition:</strong> Calvin's lesser magistrates
        (<em>Inst.</em> IV.xx.31); the <em>Vindiciae contra tyrannos</em> (1579); Knox;
        Beza; Rutherford's <em>Lex Rex</em> (1644); Mayhew's <em>Discourse Concerning
        Unlimited Submission</em> (1750) against the loyalist reading of Romans 13; and in
        the twentieth century the Barmen Declaration (1934) and Bonhoeffer.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Decisive Limit on This Passage's Applicability</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Numbers 16 adjudicates rebellion against legitimate
          and faithful authority. It contains no ruling whatever on resistance to abusive
          authority, because that case does not arise in it.</p>
          <p>The text stipulates a righteous Moses. Every charge Dathan and Abiram bring is
          false — Moses did not enrich himself, did not seek princedom, did not lead them
          out to kill them — and the narrative demonstrates this. For the other case the
          canon has its own texts, and they are not gentle: Eli's sons
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Samuel 2:12-36">1 Samuel 2:12–36</a>),
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Ezekiel 34">Ezekiel 34</a> on the shepherds who feed
          themselves, <a class="ref" data-ref="Jeremiah 23">Jeremiah 23</a>,
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Micah 3">Micah 3</a>, Matthew 23, and
          <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 5:2-3">1 Peter 5:2–3</a> on not domineering over
          the flock. <strong>Using Numbers 16 against a critic of an abusive leader is
          therefore not merely harsh; it is a category error</strong> — applying a text
          about false accusations of corruption to a situation of true ones.
          <a href="part-5.html">Part V</a> takes this up in full.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="approach">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Unauthorised Approach — A Biblical Theme</h2>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead><tr><th>Text</th><th>Act</th><th>Outcome</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Leviticus 10:1-3">Lev 10:1–3</a></td><td>Nadab and Abihu offer unauthorised fire</td><td>Fire from the LORD consumes them</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 16:35">Num 16:35</a></td><td>The 250 offer incense</td><td>Fire from the LORD consumes them</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Samuel 6:19">1 Sam 6:19</a></td><td>Beth-shemesh looks into the ark</td><td>Struck</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Samuel 6:6-7">2 Sam 6:6–7</a></td><td>Uzzah steadies the ark</td><td>Struck</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Chronicles 26:16-21">2 Chr 26:16–21</a></td><td><strong>King Uzziah</strong> takes a censer into the temple; 80 priests resist him</td><td>Leprosy</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Samuel 13:8-14">1 Sam 13:8–14</a></td><td>Saul offers the sacrifice</td><td>Kingdom torn from him</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Uzziah is the closest analogue and is rarely noticed: a legitimate, indeed a
        <em>good</em> king, whose sin is precisely a censer in the wrong hands, and whose
        resisters are priests who tell their sovereign to leave the sanctuary. The
        Chronicler's verdict — "when he was strong, he grew proud, to his destruction"
        (26:16) — is the Korah diagnosis applied to the head of state.</p>

        <div class="tg-definition">
          <span class="tg-definition__term">What the cluster is about</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__original">qarab — to draw near</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__def">The <em>qorban</em> logic — drawing near to
          lethal holiness on terms not one's own. Not obedience to leaders. Not
          institutional loyalty. <strong>Approach.</strong> Every one of these narratives is
          answered, in Christian reading, by Hebrews 10:19–22, where the terms of approach
          are supplied from the other side.</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="rod">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Rod — Vindication by Life</h2>

        <p><a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 17">Numbers 17</a> (Hebrew 17:16–26) is the
        neglected half of the story and theologically the richer one. Twelve staffs, one
        per tribe, placed before the testimony overnight. Aaron's <strong>sprouts, buds,
        blossoms, and bears ripe almonds</strong> — the whole cycle of a season compressed
        into a night. The staff is then kept in the ark
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 9:4">Hebrews 9:4</a>) "as a sign for the rebels,
        that you may make an end of their grumblings."</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">God Answers the Same Question Twice</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The first vindication was by fire and the pit — 250
          dead, and then a plague that killed 14,700 more. The second settles the identical
          question by dead wood producing fruit.</p>
          <p>God answers the same challenge twice, once by death and once by life, and it is
          the <em>second</em> sign that is preserved in the ark as the permanent memorial.</p>
        </div>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Life from dead wood</strong> — resurrection, and the cross as the tree
          that bears fruit.</li>
          <li><strong>Almond</strong> — Hebrew <em>shaqed</em>, punning on <em>shoqed</em>,
          "watching," in <a class="ref" data-ref="Jeremiah 1:11-12">Jeremiah 1:11–12</a>:
          the almond is the first tree to wake. The rod that wakes first is the sign of the
          priest who lives.</li>
          <li><strong>The rod of Jesse</strong> (<a class="ref" data-ref="Isaiah 11:1">Isaiah 11:1</a>)
          is a different Hebrew word but was long read together with it; the almond rod
          became a standard figure of the virgin birth in later Western exegesis.</li>
          <li><strong>Contents of the ark</strong> (Hebrews 9:4): manna, rod, tablets —
          sustenance, priesthood, law. All three superseded in Christ, per Hebrews' argument.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="psalter">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Korahite Psalter — The Canon's Own Answer</h2>

        <p>This is the most striking thing in the whole reception history and it is almost
        entirely absent from the polemical tradition, which is a considerable indictment of
        that tradition.</p>

        <p>The sons of Korah did not die (Numbers 26:11). They became temple gatekeepers
        and singers (1 Chronicles 6:22–38; 9:19; 26:1–19; 2 Chronicles 20:19). Eleven
        psalms bear their name: <strong>42, 44–49, 84–85, 87–88</strong>. Now read what the
        descendants of the man swallowed alive by the earth sing:</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">The Family Sings the Sentence Back</span>
          <p><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 46:2">Psalm 46:2</a></strong> — "Therefore
          we will not fear though the earth gives way, though the mountains be moved into the
          heart of the sea." <em>Their father went down when the earth gave way.</em></p>
          <p><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 49:15">Psalm 49:15</a></strong> — "But
          God will ransom my soul from the power of Sheol, for he will receive me."
          <em>Numbers 16:33 — "they went down alive into Sheol."</em></p>
          <p><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 88:4-6">Psalm 88:4–6</a></strong> (Heman
          the Ezrahite, a Korahite) — "I am counted among those who go down to the pit… you
          have put me in the depths of the pit." <em>The darkest psalm in the Psalter, sung
          by that family.</em></p>
          <p><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 84:10">Psalm 84:10</a></strong> — "I
          would rather be a doorkeeper in the house of my God than dwell in the tents of the
          wicked." <em>Korah's sin was refusing to be a doorkeeper. Numbers 16:26 — "Depart
          from the tents of these wicked men." His sons became doorkeepers and sang the
          sentence back.</em></p>
        </div>

        <p>Whether the superscriptions denote authorship or a performing guild — and the
        guild reading is more likely — the canonical effect is the same. The Psalter has
        taken the vocabulary of the judgment and put it in the mouths of the judged man's
        line as praise.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Any Treatment That Ends at Numbers 17 Has Stopped Reading Too Early</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Numbers 16 ends in fire and a swallowed household.
          The canon does not let it end there.</p>
          <p>The tradition that has used Korah for two thousand years as the final word on
          rebels has systematically ignored that Scripture itself gave the rebel's family the
          microphone, and that what they sing is that God ransoms the soul from Sheol.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

