      <div class="tg-note">
        <span class="tg-note__label">A Note on Method</span>
        This part sets out each tradition's account of itself in the strongest form its own
        best theologians would recognise, marks where the disagreement is real, and marks
        where it isn't. It does not adjudicate. A reader will notice fairly quickly that the
        material is arranged so that either side can find its own position stated without
        caricature; that is deliberate, and it is the only honest way to write this section.
        One preliminary correction: framing this as two sides is a Western habit and a
        distortion. <strong>The Orthodox East is a third party</strong> with its own account
        of both — it regards the Reformation as a quarrel internal to a communion that had
        already departed, and it holds the same conciliar and patristic sources that both
        Western parties claim. Where the East's position materially changes the picture, it
        is noted.
      </div>

      <section class="tg-section" id="problem">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Problem Restated</h2>

        <p>Korah's challenge produces, in the church, a recursive difficulty. Both Western
        communions hold, from <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 5:4">Hebrews 5:4</a>, that
        <strong>office is conferred, not seized.</strong> Neither disputes it. What they
        dispute is who does the conferring — and <em>that</em> question cannot be settled
        by the principle they share.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">The Two Charges</span>
          <p><strong>The Catholic form:</strong> the Reformers seized an office no one had
          conferred. Bishops did not ordain them into a mandate to reconstitute the church;
          they took it, on their own reading of Scripture, against the judgment of the body
          that had authority to judge. Numbers 16:3, "all the congregation are holy," is
          <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 2:9">1 Peter 2:9</a> in a rebel's mouth.</p>
          <p><strong>The Protestant form:</strong> the papacy arrogated a priesthood Christ
          has ended and a headship Christ retains. To claim a mediatorial office after
          Hebrews 7–10 is precisely to take an honour unto oneself. Korah wanted a censer;
          Rome kept one after the veil was torn.</p>
        </div>

        <p>Each charge is coherent <em>given its own premises about where the church is.</em>
        Neither charge is available to the other side as a premise. This is what a genuine
        impasse looks like, and pretending otherwise — in either direction — is a failure of
        seriousness, not a form of charity.</p>

        <p>So the operative questions become personal and practical: <strong>placement</strong>
        (on what grounds do I believe I am in the church rather than outside it?),
        <strong>assurance</strong> (on what grounds do I believe I am right with God?), and
        <strong>the other side</strong> (what do I think is happening to the person across
        the divide, and how do I treat them?).</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="catholic">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Placement — The Catholic Account</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Claim in Its Own Terms</h3>

        <p>The Catholic answer to "where is the church?" is that the Church of Christ
        <strong>subsists in</strong> the Catholic Church, governed by the successor of Peter
        and the bishops in communion with him (<em>Lumen Gentium</em> 8). The formula is
        deliberate and the drafting history matters: the schema originally read <em>est</em>
        — the Church of Christ <strong>is</strong> the Catholic Church — and the Council
        changed it, precisely so that the sentence could be true <em>and</em> the next
        sentence could also be true: that many elements of sanctification and truth are
        found outside her visible boundaries.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">The Case at Its Strongest</span>
          <p><strong>1. Christ founded a visible society, not an idea.</strong> He appointed
          twelve, gave them authority to bind and loose
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 16:19">Matthew 16:19</a>; 18:18), and prayed for
          their unity as a <em>sign</em> to the world
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 17:21">John 17:21</a>). A unity invisible to the
          world cannot be the sign John 17 describes.</p>
          <p><strong>2. That society has an office of adjudication.</strong>
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 15">Acts 15</a> shows the apostolic church settling
          a disputed question by a council whose decision binds — "it seemed good to the
          Holy Spirit and to us." The Jerusalem council did not settle the circumcision
          question by each party's reading of Scripture; it settled it by an authoritative
          determination.</p>
          <p><strong>3. The office continues.</strong> The apostles appointed successors and
          provided for succession — <em>1 Clement</em> 44, written within living memory of
          the apostles, and the same document that uses Aaron's budding rod to argue it.
          Ignatius of Antioch, a decade or so later, presupposes the monarchical episcopate
          as already normal.</p>
          <p><strong>4. Doctrine develops but does not reverse.</strong> Newman's
          <em>Essay on the Development of Christian Doctrine</em> (1845) supplies the
          criteria — preservation of type, continuity of principles, assimilative power — by
          which the church can articulate what it has always held in forms not previously
          stated.</p>
          <p><strong>5. Without such an office, disputes have no terminus.</strong> The
          Protestant world's fragmentation is not incidental; it is the predicted consequence
          of removing the adjudicating authority.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">What This Means for the Person Across the Divide</h3>

        <p>Vatican II's language is careful and worth quoting precisely, because it is
        routinely misreported in both directions. Baptised non-Catholics are
        <strong>"separated brethren"</strong> who are "in some, though imperfect, communion
        with the Catholic Church" (<em>Unitatis Redintegratio</em> 3). Their communities
        possess Scripture, faith, hope, charity, the gifts of the Spirit, and real interior
        gifts of grace, and the Spirit uses them as means of salvation. The document
        explicitly says that those born into these communities who believe in Christ
        <strong>cannot be charged with the sin of separation.</strong></p>

        <p><em>Dominus Iesus</em> (2000) then draws the line the ecumenical documents leave
        implicit: communities that have not preserved valid episcopate and the substance of
        the eucharistic mystery are <strong>not "Churches in the proper sense"</strong> but
        <em>ecclesial communities</em>. This wording caused real hurt and was received by
        many Protestants as a retraction of Vatican II; the Catholic response is that it is a
        clarification, not a reversal.</p>

        <p><em>Lumen Gentium</em> 16 goes further outward still: those who through no fault
        of their own do not know the Gospel but seek God with a sincere heart and try to do
        his will may attain salvation. Whatever <em>extra ecclesiam nulla salus</em> meant in
        Cyprian's mouth, this is not Feeneyism, and the Church condemned Feeney's rigorist
        reading in 1949.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Internal Difficulty, Stated Honestly</h3>

        <p>The Catholic account has to carry the historical weight of the very abuses that
        produced the Reformation, and its own best theologians say so.
        <em>Unitatis Redintegratio</em> 7 acknowledges that men of both sides were to blame.
        John Paul II's <em>Ut Unum Sint</em> (1995) went further than any modern document: he
        acknowledged that the papacy constitutes a difficulty for most other Christians, and
        <strong>invited the leaders and theologians of other communions to a patient dialogue
        about how the primacy might be exercised</strong> so as to accomplish what Christ
        intended. That is a striking sentence from a pope, and Protestant readers who want a
        fair picture should know it exists.</p>

        <p>The difficulty that remains is structural: the office that guarantees the
        tradition is also the office that reformed it and was reformed by it, and there is no
        vantage outside the system from which to test the guarantee. The Catholic response is
        that this is true of <em>every</em> epistemic system, including the one that grounds
        itself on a canon it received from the church, and that the demand for such a vantage
        is incoherent rather than merely unmet.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="protestant">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Placement — The Protestant Account</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Claim in Its Own Terms</h3>

        <p>The Reformers did not claim to found a church. They claimed to be the catholic
        church, cleansed — that the marks by which a church is known had been obscured and
        were being restored. <strong>Augsburg Confession VII</strong>: the church is the
        assembly of saints in which the Gospel is rightly taught and the sacraments rightly
        administered; and for true unity it is enough (<em>satis est</em>) to agree on these,
        without uniformity of human traditions. The <strong>Belgic Confession</strong> 29
        adds discipline as a third mark.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">The Case at Its Strongest</span>
          <p><strong>1. The church is constituted by the Gospel, not the Gospel by the
          church.</strong> The church is <em>creatura verbi</em>: generated by the word it
          receives, and therefore standing under it. This is why Luther at Worms could not
          recant against Scripture and clear reason — not because private judgment outranks
          the church, but because the church's own authority is derivative.</p>
          <p><strong>2. Councils and popes have in fact erred, and the church has said
          so.</strong> The Council of Constance deposed multiple claimants to the papacy;
          Honorius I was condemned by an ecumenical council; the Great Western Schism left
          Europe with rival obediences, each with its own saints. An adjudicating office that
          has itself required adjudication cannot be the final court.</p>
          <p><strong>3. The marks are checkable.</strong> Word and sacrament are visible,
          public, and testable against Scripture. A succession is not: it is a historical
          claim about invisible transmission through a chain no one can audit, and a valid
          chain can transmit a corrupt gospel.</p>
          <p><strong>4. <em>Sola scriptura</em> is not <em>solo scriptura</em>.</strong> The
          Reformers held the ecumenical creeds, cited the Fathers constantly, wrote
          confessions, and exercised church discipline. Scripture is the <em>norma normans
          non normata</em> — the norm that norms and is not normed — while creeds, councils,
          and confessions are real but subordinate authority.</p>
          <p><strong>5. Visible and invisible.</strong> Not two churches but two aspects: the
          elect known to God (2 Timothy 2:19 — the Korah verse), and the mixed visible
          assembly. This is Augustine's own settlement against the Donatists, and it means
          that no visible boundary, drawn anywhere, is identical with the boundary of the
          redeemed.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">What This Means for the Person Across the Divide</h3>

        <p>Protestant assessments of Rome span a wider range than Catholic assessments of
        Protestantism, and honesty requires showing the range rather than a median.</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Calvin</strong> held that vestiges of the church remain under the
          papacy, and — the crucial practical concession — that <strong>Roman baptism is
          valid</strong>, so that converts were not rebaptised (<em>Inst.</em> IV.ii.11–12).
          Baptism belongs to Christ, not to the administering body. Nearly all magisterial
          Protestantism has followed this.</li>
          <li><strong>The Westminster Confession</strong> (1646) 25.6 in its original text
          called the pope "that antichrist, that man of sin." American Presbyterian revisions
          (1788, and the 1903 amendments) removed the language, as have most later
          confessional bodies that retain Westminster; a few retain it. This history should
          be stated plainly rather than quietly dropped, in either direction.</li>
          <li><strong>Nineteenth-century Reformed</strong> opinion divided sharply on whether
          Rome is a true-but-corrupt church or a false one — Charles Hodge and Thornwell
          taking opposite sides in a famous exchange over the validity of Roman baptism.</li>
          <li><strong>Modern evangelicalism</strong> ranges from "our closest allies on the
          moral and creedal questions" to "a different gospel." The 1994 statement
          <em>Evangelicals and Catholics Together</em> and its 1997 follow-up
          <em>The Gift of Salvation</em> provoked serious backlash from figures including
          R. C. Sproul, John MacArthur, and Michael Horton, on the grounds that
          co-belligerency had been allowed to blur justification.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Internal Difficulty, Stated Honestly</h3>

        <p>Fragmentation. The <em>satis est</em> of Augsburg VII was intended to make unity
        achievable by lowering the required agreement to Word and sacrament; in practice,
        disagreement about what constitutes "rightly taught" and "rightly administered"
        reproduced the problem one level down. Protestants have divided over baptism, the
        supper, polity, predestination, gifts, and a great deal else, and each division was
        made by people who believed they were applying the marks.</p>

        <p>The strongest Protestant answers are that visible unity was never promised in the
        form Rome describes; that the divisions are real sin, confessed as such; and that a
        fragmented church holding the Gospel is in better condition than a unified church
        that has lost it. The strongest Catholic rejoinder is that this is an <em>ad hoc</em>
        rationalisation of a predicted failure. Neither party finds the other's answer
        satisfying, and a reader should not expect to.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="assurance">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Assurance — The Deepest Practical Divide</h2>

        <p>Justification is the famous dispute. <strong>Assurance is the one people actually
        live inside</strong>, and it is where the two systems produce visibly different
        interior lives. It also has direct Korah relevance: the men who died in Numbers 16
        were "chosen from the assembly, men of renown" — the question of whether one's
        standing is secure is raised by the text itself.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Catholic Position</h3>

        <p>Trent, Session 6 (1547), chapter 9 and canons 13–16, condemned what it called
        "the vain confidence of heretics": no one can know with the <strong>certainty of
        faith</strong>, free from all error, that he has obtained the grace of God; and no
        one can know with absolute and infallible certainty that he will persevere, absent a
        special revelation. This is regularly misreported as "Catholics have no assurance."
        What Trent actually denies is a particular <em>kind</em> of certainty about a
        particular object. What Catholic teaching affirms is substantial:</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">What Is Affirmed</span>
          <p><strong>Objective, sacramental grounds.</strong> I was baptised; I was absolved;
          I received the Eucharist. These are public, datable acts of Christ through his
          church, not readings of my own heart. The Catholic system deliberately locates the
          ground of confidence <em>outside the believer's introspection.</em></p>
          <p><strong>The theological virtue of hope</strong> — not wishful thinking but a
          settled, grace-given confidence in God's promise, which the Catechism treats as
          certain in its object even while the subject remains capable of falling. Plus
          <strong>moral certitude</strong>, the ordinary practical confidence a Christian may
          and should have.</p>
          <p><strong>The reason for the reserve:</strong> CCC 2005 — grace belongs to the
          supernatural order, escapes experience, and cannot be known except by faith. The
          reserve is aimed at <em>presumption</em>, which the tradition classes with despair
          as a sin against hope (CCC 2091–2092).</p>
        </div>

        <p>The Catholic critique of the Protestant position: a doctrine that one's
        justification is settled and unlosable invites presumption and antinomianism, cuts
        the nerve of the New Testament's own warnings
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 6:4-8">Hebrews 6:4–8</a>; 10:26–31;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 9:27">1 Corinthians 9:27</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 5:4">Galatians 5:4</a>), and, in its introspective
        forms, has produced as much anxiety as anything Rome ever did.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Protestant Position</h3>

        <p><strong>Westminster 18</strong> is the mature statement, and its structure is
        often missed. 18.1 denies assurance to hypocrites. <strong>18.2 affirms "an
        infallible assurance of faith,"</strong> founded on the divine truth of the promises,
        the inward evidence of the graces, and the testimony of the Spirit of adoption.
        <strong>18.3 then says this assurance does not belong to the essence of faith</strong>
        — a true believer may wait long and struggle much before obtaining it.</p>

        <p>That third clause records a real development. Luther and early Calvin treated
        assurance as close to the essence of faith: to believe <em>is</em> to be persuaded
        that Christ is for me. Later Puritan practical divinity, facing congregations full of
        people who did not feel persuaded, developed the <em>syllogismus practicus</em> —
        examining the fruit of one's life as evidence of regeneration. The Marrow controversy
        and Wesley's later doctrine of the witness of the Spirit are further moves in the
        same argument.</p>

        <p>The grounds offered are:
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 8:16">Romans 8:16</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 5:13">1 John 5:13</a> (written <em>that you may
        know</em>); <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 8:38-39">Romans 8:38–39</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="John 10:28-29">John 10:28–29</a>; and Heidelberg Q1, which
        frames the whole catechism as an answer to the question of comfort.</p>

        <p>The Protestant critique of the Catholic position: a system in which mortal sin
        destroys the state of grace and confession restores it makes one's standing before
        God a function of an unfinished accounting, and produces exactly the terrors Luther
        described in the monastery.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Honest Cross-Assessment</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Both traditions' healthiest streams locate assurance
          outside the self; both traditions' pathologies come from relocating it inside.</p>
          <p>The Catholic mechanism is objective and sacramental — <em>you were baptised, you
          were absolved</em> — but degrades into scrupulosity when it becomes an audit of
          whether the confession was complete, the contrition sufficient, the state of grace
          intact this hour. The Protestant mechanism is objective and forensic —
          <em>Christ's righteousness is reckoned to you, extra nos</em> — but degrades into
          morbid introspection when the practical syllogism turns the search for evidence of
          regeneration into a permanent examination of one's own sincerity.</p>
          <p><strong>Each tradition's characteristic disease is the mirror of the
          other's</strong>, and each is a failure of that tradition's own best theology rather
          than a fair consequence of it. A Protestant who is anxious because he cannot find
          enough fruit and a Catholic who is anxious because she cannot be sure of her last
          confession have arrived at the same place by opposite roads, and the road each of
          them should be on runs outside.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">What the Joint Declaration Did and Did Not Do</h3>

        <p>The <strong>Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification</strong> (Lutheran
        World Federation and the Holy See, Augsburg, 31 October 1999) states a common
        confession: that by grace alone, in faith in Christ's saving work and not because of
        any merit on our part, we are accepted by God and receive the Holy Spirit, who renews
        our hearts and calls and equips us to good works. It then declares that <strong>the
        mutual condemnations of the sixteenth century do not apply to the teaching of the
        partners as set out in the Declaration.</strong> The World Methodist Council
        associated itself in 2006, the Anglican Consultative Council in 2016, and the World
        Communion of Reformed Churches in 2017.</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">What It Did Not Do</span>
          It did not resolve the underlying disputes about merit, the nature of concupiscence
          in the baptised, indulgences, or the relation of justification to the sacramental
          system; these are named in the Declaration itself as requiring further
          clarification. It did not command consensus even within Lutheranism — a large group
          of German university theologians publicly protested that the differentiated
          consensus was verbal rather than real, and the Congregation for the Doctrine of the
          Faith's 1998 response raised its own difficulties, which the 1999 Annex was drafted
          to address. And critically: <strong>it did not touch authority.</strong> That, on
          the evidence of the last five hundred years, is the deeper divide.
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="real">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Where the Disagreement Is Real</h2>

        <p>A serious treatment must resist two opposite temptations: inflating the
        differences into a caricature, and deflating them into a misunderstanding. The
        following are genuine and unresolved.</p>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead><tr><th>Locus</th><th>Catholic</th><th>Protestant (magisterial)</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Authority</strong></td><td>Scripture and Tradition as one deposit, with a living magisterium that serves and does not stand above the word (<em>Dei Verbum</em> 9–10)</td><td>Scripture as the sole final norm; creeds and confessions as subordinate, real authority</td><td><strong>The root divide.</strong> Everything else is downstream</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Papacy</strong></td><td>Universal ordinary jurisdiction; infallibility <em>ex cathedra</em> (Vatican I, 1870)</td><td>Denied; primacy of honour at most. Orthodoxy also denies, on different grounds</td><td>Hardest single obstacle; <em>Ut Unum Sint</em> 95–96 opened discussion of its <em>exercise</em></td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Eucharist</strong></td><td>Transubstantiation; the Mass as the one sacrifice of Calvary made present, not repeated (CCC 1367)</td><td>Range: Lutheran sacramental union, Reformed spiritual real presence, Zwinglian memorial</td><td>Real, though "re-presented not repeated" removes a common misfire</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Orders</strong></td><td>Sacramental priesthood; Anglican orders declared null (1896)</td><td>Ministry of Word and sacrament, conferred by call and ordination, not sacerdotal in kind</td><td>Real; ARCIC made progress that has not been officially received</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Justification</strong></td><td>Grace-initiated, transformative, involving genuine interior renewal; merit itself a gift (CCC 2010)</td><td>Forensic imputation of Christ's righteousness; sanctification distinguished though never separated</td><td><strong>Narrowed substantially</strong> by the Joint Declaration; residue concerns merit and imputation/impartation</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Marian dogmas</strong></td><td>Immaculate Conception (1854), Assumption (1950), defined as binding</td><td>Rejected as lacking scriptural warrant and as improperly defined</td><td>Real, and doubly so — about the doctrines <em>and</em> the authority to define them</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Purgatory / indulgences</strong></td><td>Final purification of the saved (CCC 1030–32); indulgences reformed but retained</td><td>Rejected</td><td>Real</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Canon</strong></td><td>Deuterocanonicals as Scripture (Trent, Session 4, 1546)</td><td>Excluded; Article VI allows them for example of life, not doctrine</td><td>Real, and older than the Reformation — Jerome held the distinction; Orthodoxy has a third list</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Root</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The deepest disagreement is not about justification.
          It is about how disagreements are settled.</p>
          <p>Which is exactly the question Numbers 16 raises and — as Part I concluded —
          cannot answer, because any use of it presupposes a prior answer. A Catholic and a
          Protestant who agree on the entire content of the Joint Declaration still disagree
          about what would happen if they came to disagree. That is the divide, and it is why
          five centuries of narrowing on soteriology has not produced union.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="notreal">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Where the Disagreement Is Not Real</h2>

        <p>Equally important, and more often botched. These are misrepresentations, and each
        side owes the other their removal.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">What Catholics Do Not Teach, Contrary to Common Protestant Claim</span>
          <p><em>That we earn salvation by works.</em> The Second Council of Orange (529)
          condemned semi-Pelagianism; Trent's own decree on justification opens from
          prevenient grace; CCC 2010 states that no one can merit the initial grace of
          forgiveness and justification. Merit in Catholic theology is God crowning his own
          gifts — Augustine's formula, retained.</p>
          <p><em>That Mary is worshipped.</em> The tradition distinguishes <em>latria</em>
          (worship, God alone) from <em>dulia</em> (honour to saints) and <em>hyperdulia</em>
          (the higher honour to Mary). One may think the distinction pastorally unstable in
          practice — that is a real argument — but claiming the doctrine teaches worship of
          Mary is simply inaccurate.</p>
          <p><em>That the Mass re-sacrifices Christ.</em> CCC 1367 states that the sacrifice
          of Christ and the sacrifice of the Eucharist are one single sacrifice. Protestants
          may judge this incoherent; they should not describe it as a repetition, because the
          Catechism explicitly denies that.</p>
          <p><em>That Scripture is subordinate to the church.</em> <em>Dei Verbum</em> 10
          states that the magisterium is not above the word of God but serves it.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">What Protestants Do Not Teach, Contrary to Common Catholic Claim</span>
          <p><em>That every believer is his own pope.</em> The confessions, the creeds,
          ordination, church discipline, and the subordinate authority of the church are all
          constitutive of magisterial Protestantism. <em>Solo scriptura</em> is the position
          of some radical and restorationist movements, and the confessional traditions have
          opposed it from the beginning.</p>
          <p><em>That works are irrelevant.</em>
          <a class="ref" data-ref="James 2">James 2</a> is in every Protestant canon. The
          formula is that we are justified by faith alone, but the faith that justifies is
          never alone.</p>
          <p><em>That the church is invisible and therefore does not matter.</em> The
          distinction is between two aspects, and Calvin called the visible church our mother,
          outside which there is no ordinary possibility of salvation
          (<em>Inst.</em> IV.i.4) — a sentence startling to most modern evangelicals.</p>
          <p><em>That there was no church between the apostles and Luther.</em> The Reformers
          cited Augustine, Chrysostom, Cyprian, and Bernard constantly; they claimed
          continuity and argued the innovation was on the other side.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-definition">
          <span class="tg-definition__term">The steel-manning test</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__def">State the other side's position, and then ask
          whether a well-catechised member of it would say <em>"yes, that is what I hold."</em>
          If not, the position being attacked is not theirs, and any argument against it is
          wasted — including any argument that they are Korah.</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="charge">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Korah Charge Between the Two</h2>

        <p>Both sides have thrown this text at each other for five hundred years. What can
        actually be said now:</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Four Findings</span>
          <p><strong>1. The charge cannot be self-verifying.</strong> As Part II established,
          Amaziah brought exactly the Korah accusation against Amos and was wrong. A charge of
          rebellion made <em>by</em> the challenged party, in its own cause, has no
          evidentiary force, because both sides can make it and both do.</p>
          <p><strong>2. Both sides pass the shared test.</strong> Neither Catholic priests nor
          Protestant ministers are self-appointed. Each is examined, called, and ordained by a
          body with a considered account of its own authority to do so. The Hebrews 5:4
          principle is satisfied on both sides <em>by their own lights</em>, and each side's
          denial that the other's conferral is valid is a denial of the other's
          <em>ecclesiology</em>, not evidence of self-seizure by the individual minister.</p>
          <p><strong>3. The censers cut against maximalism in both directions.</strong>
          Numbers 17:2–3: the instruments of illicit worship "have become holy" and are
          hammered onto the altar as a permanent memorial. It is the Old Testament's nearest
          anticipation of the Augustinian settlement that the sacrament belongs to Christ and
          not to the minister — the settlement that lets Rome recognise Protestant baptism and
          lets Calvin refuse to rebaptise converts from Rome.</p>
          <p><strong>4. Numbers 26:11 is in the canon.</strong> The sons of Korah did not die.
          Whatever verdict falls on a party's leaders, it does not automatically fall on those
          born into it. This is precisely <em>Unitatis Redintegratio</em> 3's point, arrived at
          by another road.</p>
        </div>

        <p>One further datum should be stated without softening. <strong>In Numbers 16 the
        leader under challenge never names his challengers.</strong> Moses falls on his face,
        says the verdict will come in the morning from God, and then intercedes for the lives
        of the people accusing him. The narrative supplies no example of the challenged party
        successfully identifying the rebel; it supplies a theophany, precisely because human
        adjudication in one's own cause was refused.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="personal">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Personal Question</h2>

        <p>The abstract version is "what is the status of the other communion." The version
        people actually ask at two in the morning is: <strong>if I am on the wrong side of
        this, am I lost?</strong> Each tradition has an answer, and both answers are more
        generous than their polemics.</p>

        <p><strong>The Catholic answer to a Protestant:</strong> you are baptised, and baptism
        incorporates into Christ. You are a separated brother or sister in real if imperfect
        communion. Your faith, hope, and charity are genuine gifts of the Spirit; your
        community is used by the Spirit as a means of salvation; you are not charged with the
        sin of a separation you did not make. Whether you should regularise is a different
        question from whether you are in danger.</p>

        <p><strong>The Protestant answer to a Catholic:</strong> varies, and both poles should
        be named. The mainstream magisterial answer, from Calvin forward, is that Rome is a
        church in which the marks are gravely obscured but not wholly absent, that its baptism
        is valid, that the Apostles' and Nicene Creeds are confessed there, and that a person
        who trusts Christ for salvation is saved wherever he stands. The harder answer, held
        by a minority, is that a system teaching justification otherwise preaches another
        gospel and its adherents are in danger. Readers should know both answers exist and
        should not be told only the one they find comfortable.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">What Both Traditions Say Structurally</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">God is not bound by the boundary, and the person is
          not saved by having drawn it correctly.</p>
          <p>2 Timothy 2:19 — quoting Numbers 16:5 — is the governing text for exactly this
          situation. <em>The Lord knows those who are his.</em> Paul cites the Korah story not
          to sort the assembly but to say that the sorting is God's, and that the believer's
          business in the meantime is to depart from iniquity. <strong>The New Testament's own
          use of Numbers 16 is an argument against the confident identification of who is truly
          in.</strong></p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">A Pastoral Note</span>
          Anxiety about being on the wrong side of a church division is common, real, and
          often severe, and it responds poorly to more argument. A person in that condition
          should talk to someone — a pastor, a priest, a spiritual director, a trusted friend
          who knows them — rather than trying to settle five centuries of contested ecclesiology
          alone at night. The tradition's own counsel on scrupulosity, in both communions, is
          uniformly that it is not resolved by further private investigation.
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="living">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Living With the Other Side</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">What Is Actually Permitted</h3>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Baptism</strong> — mutually recognised in most cases. Rome accepts
          Trinitarian baptism with water in the proper form; nearly all magisterial Protestants
          accept Roman baptism.</li>
          <li><strong>Eucharist</strong> — not shared, and this is the sharpest daily pain.
          Catholic canon law (canon 844) permits reception by non-Catholics only in danger of
          death or grave necessity, and requires that the recipient hold Catholic eucharistic
          faith. A Protestant should understand that a Catholic declining to commune at his
          church is not a snub but obedience; a Catholic should understand that the Protestant
          experiences the closed table as a judgment on his baptism, and should not be surprised
          that it wounds.</li>
          <li><strong>Marriage</strong> — permitted with dispensation; the Catholic party
          undertakes to do what they can to raise the children Catholic. This deserves to be
          settled before the wedding rather than after.</li>
          <li><strong>Common prayer and witness</strong> — encouraged by both. The Week of
          Prayer for Christian Unity, joint social work, disaster relief, and shared advocacy
          are all ordinary.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">A Rule of Engagement</span>
          <p><strong>1. Learn the other side from its own sources.</strong> Read the Catechism,
          <em>Lumen Gentium</em>, and <em>Dei Verbum</em>; read the Westminster Confession, the
          Augsburg Confession, and the Heidelberg Catechism. Not summaries by opponents. This is
          the single highest-yield discipline available and almost no one does it.</p>
          <p><strong>2. Argue the strongest version.</strong> If your account of the other side
          would embarrass their best theologian, you are arguing with someone who does not exist.</p>
          <p><strong>3. Keep validity and liceity distinct.</strong> Most cross-divide argument
          conflates "this is irregular" with "this does nothing."</p>
          <p><strong>4. Do not use Korah.</strong> It cannot decide the question, it presupposes
          the answer, and it converts a disagreement into an accusation of damnable rebellion.
          The one thing it does yield — that office is conferred, not seized — is affirmed by
          both sides.</p>
          <p><strong>5. Notice which criticisms come from inside.</strong> A tradition's
          self-criticism is where its real vulnerabilities are, and it is more useful than
          anything an opponent has written.</p>
          <p><strong>6. Distinguish the system from the person.</strong> A believer's relation
          to Christ is not a function of their communion's dogmatic accuracy. Both traditions
          concede this in their own documents.</p>
          <p><strong>7. Do not pretend the disagreement is verbal.</strong> False irenicism is
          its own failure of respect: it tells the other side their convictions are not worth
          taking seriously.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="lands">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Where Numbers 16 Actually Lands</h2>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">What Korah's Rebellion Says to Anyone Standing Anywhere</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Approach is on God's terms. A true premise can be
          weaponised. Office is conferred, not seized. The mediator is the point. The verdict
          belongs to God. And the story does not end at the judgment.</p>
          <p><strong>Approach is on God's terms.</strong> The question of Numbers 16 is
          <em>who may draw near</em>, and the answer is never <em>whoever asserts it</em>. This
          falls equally on a Protestant who treats access to God as a private entitlement and a
          Catholic who treats sacramental participation as an automatic operation.</p>
          <p><strong>A true premise can be weaponised.</strong> "All the congregation are holy"
          is Exodus 19:6, and it destroyed the man who said it. Both traditions hold true
          doctrines that their members deploy for their own elevation. This is the most
          uncomfortable thing in the chapter and the least preached.</p>
          <p><strong>The mediator is the point.</strong> Aaron between the dead and the living,
          with a censer — the intercessor standing inside the judgment, holding the means of
          atonement. Not the defender of a position. That figure is Christ
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 7:25">Hebrews 7:25</a>), and both communions confess
          him in the same words.</p>
          <p><strong>And the story does not end at the judgment.</strong> The sons of Korah did
          not die. They kept the doors of the house of God, and they sang: <em>God will ransom
          my soul from the power of Sheol</em>
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 49:15">Psalm 49:15</a>).</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-application">
          <span class="tg-application__label">A Closing Observation</span>
          <p>Numbers 16 answered its question by fire and by an opened earth, and then answered
          the same question a second time with a dead stick that put out almonds overnight. God
          settled the identical dispute twice, once by death and once by life — and it was the
          living sign, not the fire, that was placed in the ark to be kept.</p>
          <p><strong>Five hundred years of Western Christians using this chapter against each
          other have drawn almost exclusively on the fire.</strong></p>
        </div>
      </section>

