      </section>

      <section class="tg-part" id="part-5" aria-labelledby="part-5-h">
        <div class="tg-part__head">
          <span class="tg-part__kicker">Part V of Five</span>
          <h2 class="tg-part__title" id="part-5-h">Spiritual Abuse: Korah, Jeremiah, and the Use of a Text as a Muzzle</h2>
          <p class="tg-part__intro">Somewhere this week, someone raised a concern about a leader and was told they had
        "a spirit of Korah." That sentence is this passage's most frequent real-world
        application, and it is almost always a misuse. But "almost always" is not "always,"
        and a treatment that simply inverts the polemic — making every leader Eli and every
        critic Jeremiah — has only changed which side gets to skip the argument.</p>
          <blockquote class="tg-part__verse"><p>Woe to the shepherds of Israel who have been feeding themselves! … With force and
           harshness you have ruled them.</p>
        <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Ezekiel 34:2">Ezekiel 34:2</a>, 4</cite></blockquote>
        </div>
<nav class="tg-toc tg-toc--sticky" aria-label="Part contents">
