      <div class="tg-note">
        <span class="tg-note__label">A Note on Method, and a Caution</span>
        This part does three things. It sets out what the canon actually says about leaders
        who harm the people they lead, which is a great deal more than is usually preached.
        It supplies the discernment work that Part II named as the tradition's weakest point
        — a working test rather than an assertion of categories. And it takes seriously the
        position of the person on the receiving end, because the Psalter has more to say to
        them than the commentaries do, and it says it in the voice of Korah's own sons.<br><br>
        <strong>Nothing here is a diagnosis of any particular church, and no reader should
        use it as one.</strong> The material is arranged so that a leader examining himself
        and a member examining a leader can both use it honestly — which means it will be
        uncomfortable in both directions, and it is meant to be.
      </div>

      <section class="tg-section" id="necessary">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Why This Part Is Necessary</h2>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Finding Everything Below Depends On</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Numbers 16 stipulates a righteous Moses. The chapter
          adjudicates rebellion against legitimate and faithful authority. It contains no
          ruling whatever on resistance to abusive authority, because that case does not
          arise in it.</p>
          <p>Every charge Dathan and Abiram bring is false. Moses did not enrich himself —
          "I have not taken one donkey from them"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 16:15">Numbers 16:15</a>). He did not seek
          princedom. He did not lead them out to kill them. The narrative demonstrates this,
          and the demonstration is what makes the judgment just.</p>
          <p><strong>Applying it to a critic of an abusive leader is not merely harsh. It is
          a category error</strong> — using a text about <em>false</em> accusations of
          corruption to settle a situation of <em>true</em> ones.</p>
        </div>

        <p>For the case that does not arise in Numbers 16, the canon has its own texts. They
        are extensive, they are severe, and they are conspicuously absent from most preaching
        on authority. Setting them out is the first task.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="definition">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Definition — What Spiritual Abuse Is, and What It Isn't</h2>

        <div class="tg-definition">
          <span class="tg-definition__term">Spiritual abuse</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__def">The use of religious authority, teaching, or
          standing to control, coerce, or exploit a person, in a way that damages their
          spiritual and personal wellbeing — characteristically by placing the leader, the
          institution, or the leader's approval in the position that belongs to God. Its
          distinguishing feature is not severity but <strong>mechanism</strong>: the harm is
          done <em>through</em> the person's faith, which is why it wounds where ordinary
          mistreatment does not, and why the ordinary remedies are so hard to reach for.</span>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Term Is Contested, and Honestly So</h3>

        <p><strong>The case for it.</strong> The mechanism really is distinct. Being told
        that leaving your church means leaving God, or that your doubt is demonic, or that
        reporting a crime is gossip, does something that a rude employer cannot do. Lisa
        Oakley's empirical work, and the Church of England's decision to name the category in
        its safeguarding practice, rest on that distinctiveness.</p>

        <p><strong>The case against it.</strong> Critics — including some safeguarding
        professionals — argue the term is elastic enough to be weaponised in turn, that it can
        convert any unwelcome church discipline into an accusation, and that its lack of a
        legal or clinical definition makes it hard to adjudicate fairly.</p>

        <p>Both concerns are real, and the second is the mirror image of the first. A term
        that exists to stop one kind of unanswerable accusation should not become another.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Not Spiritual Abuse</span>
          <p><strong>Teaching that convicts.</strong> A sermon that makes you uncomfortable
          because it is true is doing its job. "All Scripture is… profitable for reproof, for
          correction" (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 3:16">2 Timothy 3:16</a>).</p>
          <p><strong>Church discipline properly conducted.</strong>
          <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 5">1 Corinthians 5</a> exists. So does
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 18:15-20">Matthew 18:15–20</a>. Discipline that is
          private first, escalated slowly, aimed at restoration, applied without partiality,
          and open to appeal is the biblical procedure, not an abuse of it.</p>
          <p><strong>Being told no.</strong> Not being given a platform, a position, or a
          hearing on one's own timetable is not oppression. Korah, notably, had a real and
          honoured office.</p>
          <p><strong>Disagreement, even sharp disagreement.</strong>
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 2:11">Galatians 2:11</a> is in the canon.</p>
          <p><strong>A leader defending a doctrine.</strong> Contending for the faith is a
          duty (<a class="ref" data-ref="Jude 3">Jude 3</a>). Contending for oneself is a
          different act.</p>
          <p><strong>The line falls at coercion and at substitution</strong> — where obedience
          to God is collapsed into obedience to a man, where the leader's approval functions as
          the sacrament, and where the exits are closed.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="muzzle">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Weaponised Text — How Korah Became a Muzzle</h2>

        <p>The rhetorical pattern is documented and recognisable. It runs roughly like this:</p>

        <ol>
          <li>A concern is raised — about money, conduct, doctrine, or the treatment of
          someone.</li>
          <li>The concern is <strong>reclassified</strong> as a spiritual condition rather
          than a claim with a truth value: <em>a spirit of Korah, an Absalom spirit, a Jezebel
          spirit, a critical spirit, division.</em></li>
          <li>The reclassification makes the concern <strong>unanswerable</strong>, because a
          spirit cannot be investigated. Evidence becomes irrelevant; the person's interior
          state is now the subject.</li>
          <li><strong>Denial of the charge confirms it.</strong> Insisting you have a
          legitimate concern is precisely what a rebellious spirit would do.</li>
          <li>The congregation is warned. Contact is limited. The person leaves or is removed
          — and their leaving is then cited as proof (<em>they went out from us</em>,
          <a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 2:19">1 John 2:19</a>, wrenched out of a passage
          about denying Christ).</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Steps 3 and 4 Are the Tell</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">A charge that cannot in principle be disconfirmed is
          not a finding; it is a device.</p>
          <p>In the text itself, the question <em>who is legitimate?</em> is settled by a
          public ordeal with a stated outcome, announced in advance, and adjudicated by God. It
          is not settled by a leader's assessment of a critic's heart.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">The Structural Objection, Which Is Decisive</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">In Numbers 16, Moses never calls anyone Korah.</div>
          <p>At the accusation he <strong>falls on his face</strong> (16:4). He says "in the
          morning the LORD will show who is his" (16:5) — deferring the verdict, to a specified
          time, to God. He sends for Dathan and Abiram to talk, and they refuse him (16:12).
          When God proposes to destroy the congregation, Moses and Aaron fall down and
          <strong>argue God out of it</strong> (16:22) — on behalf of the people who are
          attacking them. When the plague breaks out, Aaron runs <em>into</em> it with a censer
          (16:47–48).</p>
          <p>Four times under accusation, the vindicated leaders do the same thing: they go
          down, and they intercede for their accusers' lives.</p>
          <p><strong>The verdict comes from outside the dispute.</strong> A leader who names
          his critics Korah has made himself judge in his own cause — the one role Moses
          conspicuously refuses. He has not applied the passage. He has taken the position the
          passage denies him.</p>
        </div>

        <p>There is a second structural point, less often noticed. <strong>Korah had an office
        and wanted a higher one.</strong> He was a Kohathite, entrusted with the most holy
        things (<a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 4:4-15">Numbers 4:4–15</a>). His coalition was
        250 "chieftains of the congregation, chosen from the assembly, men of renown" (16:2).
        This was an insider movement of the well-placed against the top of the hierarchy —
        which is to say the Korah analogy, applied strictly, describes ambitious subordinates
        in leadership far better than it describes an ordinary member with a complaint. Jude
        saw this: his targets are people who have crept in among the love feasts and "shepherd
        themselves" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Jude 12">Jude 12</a>) — false <em>teachers</em>,
        insiders with influence, not congregants with questions.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="prooftexts">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Companion Proof-Texts, and What They Actually Say</h2>

        <p>Korah rarely travels alone. The cluster is stable enough to list, and each member
        of it fails on examination in a specific way worth knowing.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">"Touch Not the Lord's Anointed"</span>
          <p><a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 105:15">Psalm 105:15</a> =
          <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Chronicles 16:22">1 Chronicles 16:22</a>. The verse refers
          to <strong>the patriarchs</strong> — wandering sojourners whom God protected from
          <em>kings</em>. It is God warning the powerful not to harm the vulnerable. Its modern
          use exactly reverses the parties.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">David and Saul</span>
          <p><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Samuel 24">1 Samuel 24</a>;
          <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Samuel 26">26</a>. David refuses to <strong>kill</strong>
          Saul. That is the whole of the restraint. Immediately afterward he stands on a
          hillside and rebukes Saul at length and in public (24:9–15), calling his conduct what
          it is. The text forbids regicide; it explicitly models loud criticism.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Absalom</span>
          <p><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Samuel 15">2 Samuel 15</a>. Absalom mounted an
          <strong>armed coup</strong> against his father, took the throne, and publicly violated
          the king's concubines. The relevant analogy is a violent seizure of office, not a
          complaint to a board. Note also that Absalom's stated grievance — "there is no one
          appointed by the king to hear you" (15:3) — was, as a description of the judicial
          backlog, apparently accurate. He exploited a real failure. That is worth sitting with
          rather than skipping.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Miriam and Aaron</span>
          <p><a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 12">Numbers 12</a>, often cited as proof that
          criticising a leader brings judgment. What the chapter actually contains: Moses does
          not answer the criticism, God defends him (12:6–8), and when Miriam is struck
          <strong>Moses immediately intercedes for her</strong> — "O God, please heal her"
          (12:13). Again: the leader neither prosecutes nor gloats. And the fact that only
          Miriam is struck, while Aaron speaks first in the Hebrew word order and goes
          unpunished, is a genuine interpretive problem that the muzzling use never mentions.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Noah's Nakedness</span>
          <p><a class="ref" data-ref="Genesis 9:20-27">Genesis 9:20–27</a>, used to teach that
          a leader's sin must be <em>covered</em>. The passage is a notorious crux — the curse
          falls on Canaan, not Ham, for reasons the text does not explain — and it has one of
          the ugliest reception histories in Scripture. It cannot bear the weight of a policy on
          reporting misconduct, and the attempt to make it do so should be treated as a warning
          sign in itself.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">"Obey Your Leaders and Submit to Them"</span>
          <p><a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 13:17">Hebrews 13:17</a> is real, and not to be
          explained away — the verbs are <em>peithesthe</em> (be persuaded by, yield to) and
          <em>hypeikete</em> (submit). But the same sentence supplies the ground and the limit:
          leaders "keep watch over your souls, as those who will have to give an account." The
          authority is a stewardship under audit. And the immediately preceding verse, 13:7,
          tells the readers to <strong>consider the outcome of their leaders' way of life</strong>
          and imitate <em>that</em> — which is an instruction to evaluate.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">"Do Not Judge / Do Not Gossip"</span>
          <p>Slander is real sin (<a class="ref" data-ref="James 4:11">James 4:11</a>) and false
          witness is forbidden. But "the one who states his case first seems right, until the
          other comes and examines him"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Proverbs 18:17">Proverbs 18:17</a>) exists precisely to
          protect the second speaker's right to be heard. A system in which naming a fact is
          automatically gossip has abolished the possibility of accountability, and has done it
          with a proverb that says the opposite.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">"Jezebel"</span>
          <p>In <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 2:20">Revelation 2:20</a> the charge at
          Thyatira concerns a <em>teacher</em> leading people into idolatry and immorality. In
          modern usage the label is applied with striking frequency to women who ask questions.
          That distribution is itself evidence about what the label is doing.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="canon">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Canon's Own Case Against Abusive Leaders</h2>

        <p>This is the material Numbers 16 does not contain, and it is not marginal. God's
        harshest sustained language in Scripture is directed not at the congregation but at its
        shepherds.</p>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead><tr><th>Text</th><th>Charge</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Samuel 2:12-36">1 Sam 2:12–36</a></td><td>Eli's sons take the offering <strong>by force</strong> and exploit the women at the entrance; Eli rebukes weakly and is told he <strong>honoured his sons above God</strong> (2:29). The house is ended</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Isaiah 56:10-12">Isa 56:10–12</a></td><td>Watchmen who are blind, dogs who cannot bark, shepherds with no understanding — "all of them turn to their own way, each to his own gain"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Jeremiah 23:1-4">Jer 23:1–4</a></td><td>"Woe to the shepherds who destroy and scatter the sheep of my pasture… Behold, I will attend to you for your evil deeds"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Ezekiel 34:1-10">Ezek 34:1–10</a></td><td>Shepherds who feed themselves; the weak not strengthened, the sick not healed, the injured not bound up, the strayed not sought; "with force and harshness you have ruled them." God: "I am against the shepherds"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Micah 3:1-3">Mic 3:1–3</a>, <a class="ref" data-ref="Micah 3:11">3:11</a></td><td>Rulers who "tear the skin from off my people"; priests who teach for a price and prophets who divine for money — <em>and all of them lean on the LORD</em></td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Zechariah 11:15-17">Zech 11:15–17</a></td><td>The worthless shepherd who will not care for the perishing but devours the fat ones. "Woe to my worthless shepherd, who deserts the flock!"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 23">Matt 23</a></td><td>Heavy burdens laid on others' shoulders and not lifted with a finger; love of the best seats; "you shut the kingdom of heaven in people's faces" (23:13)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="John 9:34">John 9:22, 34</a></td><td>A man is <strong>expelled from the synagogue</strong> for testifying to what happened to him. "And they cast him out"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Corinthians 11:20">2 Cor 11:20</a></td><td>"For you bear it if someone makes slaves of you, or devours you, or takes advantage of you, or puts on airs, or strikes you in the face." A checklist, addressed to the tolerant congregation</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="3 John 9-10">3 John 9–10</a></td><td><strong>Diotrephes</strong> — "who likes to put himself first" — does not acknowledge apostolic authority, talks wicked nonsense, refuses the brothers, and <strong>puts out of the church</strong> those who would receive them</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Peter 5:2-3">1 Pet 5:2–3</a></td><td>Shepherd the flock "not under compulsion… not for shameful gain… not domineering (κατακυριεύοντες) over those in your charge, but being examples"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Titus 1:11">Titus 1:11</a></td><td>Those "upsetting whole households by teaching for shameful gain what they ought not to teach"</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Three Observations That Change the Shape of the Discussion</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Scripture's severest denunciations of religious figures
          are aimed downward from God at leaders, not sideways from leaders at members.</p>
          <p><strong>1. The direction of the woes.</strong> There is no passage in which God
          threatens a congregation for questioning a shepherd. There are a dozen in which he
          threatens shepherds for how they handled the flock.</p>
          <p><strong>2. Excommunication as the weapon.</strong> John 9:34 and 3 John 10 both
          record the church's own disciplinary machinery being turned against the person telling
          the truth. The Bible anticipated this specific failure and named it twice.</p>
          <p><strong>3. <a class="ref" data-ref="James 3:1">James 3:1</a>.</strong> "Not many of
          you should become teachers, my brothers, for you know that we who teach will be judged
          with greater strictness." The asymmetry of accountability runs opposite to the
          asymmetry of power — which is exactly what Numbers 16 says too, when read to the end:
          <strong>the priests bear the iniquity of the sanctuary</strong>
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 18:1">Numbers 18:1</a>). Aaron's charter, the
          settlement of the whole Korah affair, ends by loading liability onto the office, not
          privilege.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="test">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Korah or Jeremiah? A Working Test</h2>

        <p>Part II identified this as the practical crux and noted that the tradition has been
        much better at asserting the categories than supplying tests. What follows is an attempt
        at the test, built from criteria the tradition has actually offered
        (<em>m. Avot</em> 5:17; <a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 13:1-5">Deuteronomy 13:1–5</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 5:38-39">Acts 5:38–39</a>) plus the structural features of
        Numbers 16 itself.</p>

        <p><strong>The problem it has to solve:</strong> prophets attack the sanctuary
        establishment too, and they are commended for it. Amaziah laid the Korah charge — "Amos
        has conspired against you in the midst of the house of Israel"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Amos 7:10">Amos 7:10</a>) — in the exact technical vocabulary
        of sedition, against a true prophet, and he was wrong. So the charge cannot be
        self-verifying, or Amaziah wins.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Seven Questions — None Decides Alone; Together They Discriminate</span>
          <p><strong>1. What is being sought — remedy, or the office?</strong> Korah wanted the
          priesthood. Jeremiah wanted the temple to be what it claimed to be and got a cistern
          for it. <em>The sharpest single test available.</em> Ask what would satisfy the
          complainant. If the answer is "he stops doing X" or "she is made safe" or "the books
          are opened," that is reform. If the answer is "and then I take his place," that is a
          succession fight wearing a conscience.</p>
          <p><strong>2. Is the claim checkable?</strong> Prophetic critique names conduct: you
          took the offering by force, you teach for money, you devoured widows' houses. Korahite
          ambition names status: <em>why do you exalt yourselves?</em> A specific, falsifiable
          allegation invites investigation. A grievance about the leader's standing invites
          nothing. <em>This test cuts both ways, and should be applied to the accusation of
          Korah-ism too</em> — "you have a rebellious spirit" is unfalsifiable by construction.</p>
          <p><strong>3. Was the private route taken first, where it was safe to take it?</strong>
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 18:15">Matthew 18:15</a> requires the private approach
          and aims at "you have gained your brother." Korah's method was the reverse: coalition
          before conversation, maximum publicity first, displacement as the goal.
          <strong>The qualifier matters and is not optional.</strong> Matthew 18 governs "if your
          brother sins against you" — a dispute between peers aimed at restoration. It was never
          designed for crimes, for a power differential, or for cases where private confrontation
          means sending a victim back to a perpetrator. The same chapter that says <em>go
          privately</em> opens with a millstone for whoever causes one of these little ones to
          stumble (18:6). Read the chapter whole.</p>
          <p><strong>4. Who is exposed by the speaking?</strong> Korah organised 250 chieftains;
          the risk was distributed and the upside was his. Jeremiah went to the cistern, Amos was
          expelled, Micaiah got bread and water, the man in John 9 lost his synagogue.
          <strong>Whistleblowing is normally costly to the whistleblower and cheap to no
          one.</strong> Where the critic stands to gain position, standing, or audience, that fact
          belongs in the assessment — of both parties.</p>
          <p><strong>5. Does the critique conform to prior revelation, or reach past it?</strong>
          Deuteronomy 13:1–5: even a sign-working prophet is false if the content leads away from
          God. Content outranks power, credentials, and results. A leader's anointing does not
          validate his teaching, and a critic's suffering does not validate his.</p>
          <p><strong>6. What does the manner look like?</strong> <em>m. Avot</em> 5:17: is the
          dispute "for the sake of Heaven"? The category condemned is not disagreement but
          disagreement of a certain kind. Contempt, relish, escalation for its own sake, and the
          recruiting of an audience are all data. So — symmetrically — are stonewalling,
          counterattack, and the recruiting of a <em>pulpit</em>.</p>
          <p><strong>7. Who is judging?</strong> The single most reliable indicator.
          <strong>Is the verdict being rendered by the party whose position is challenged?</strong>
          Moses referred the question outward, to a public ordeal, on a stated timetable,
          adjudicated by God. Where the accused controls the investigation, selects the
          investigators, sets the terms, and announces the result, the outcome carries no
          evidentiary weight — whichever way it goes.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Honest Residue</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">These tests do not always resolve, and it is more honest
          to say so than to pretend otherwise.</p>
          <p>Real cases are mixed. Someone can raise a genuine grievance from partly
          self-interested motives; Absalom's complaint about the judicial backlog was, as far as
          the text says, true. A leader can be both faithful and badly wrong about one thing. A
          critic can be right about the facts and corrosive in the manner. And there is a tension
          internal to Numbers 16 that no one has fully harmonised: Deuteronomy 13 says a miracle
          cannot settle a doctrinal question, and Numbers 16 settles its question by miraculous
          ordeal.</p>
          <p>Which leaves <strong>Gamaliel</strong> (Acts 5:38–39) — slow, unsatisfying, and the
          most honest criterion the tradition has: some verdicts are not available yet. "Do not
          pronounce judgment before the time"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 4:5">1 Corinthians 4:5</a>).</p>
          <p><strong>The practical consequence is not paralysis. It is that protective action
          does not require a verdict.</strong> One can remove a person from access, open the
          books, involve outside authority, and suspend an office without having settled anyone's
          soul. The church habitually reverses this: it renders confident verdicts on hearts and
          takes no protective action at all.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="mechanics">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Mechanics — How These Systems Actually Work</h2>

        <p>The clinical and investigative literature converges on a set of features. They are
        listed here descriptively, not as a checklist to be scored — any one of them can occur in
        a healthy church on a bad day. It is the <em>pattern</em>, sustained and defended, that
        identifies a system.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Five Domains</span>
          <p><strong>Authority.</strong> Obedience to the leader is presented as obedience to
          God. Decisions belong to leadership in matters Scripture leaves free. Access to God's
          guidance is mediated: <em>check with your covering.</em> Questions are treated as
          symptoms.</p>
          <p><strong>Information.</strong> Facts about the institution are managed. Concerns must
          go "through the proper channels," which terminate in the person concerned. Those who
          leave are characterised <em>for</em> the congregation, who never hear them directly.
          Non-disclosure agreements accompany settlements.</p>
          <p><strong>Belonging.</strong> Membership is conditional on agreement, and the condition
          is unstated until tested. Relationships are institutional assets: leaving costs the
          friendships. Shunning is practised under other names.</p>
          <p><strong>Exit.</strong> There is no honourable way out. Departure is disloyalty,
          backsliding, or deception. A member's decision to attend elsewhere is treated as a
          spiritual failure rather than a change of address.</p>
          <p><strong>Language.</strong> A closed vocabulary makes objection incoherent from inside
          — <em>covering, touch not, spirit of Korah, unteachable, offended, bitterness, gossip,
          divisive, attacking the man of God.</em> Note how many of these convert an allegation
          about the leader's conduct into a diagnosis of the speaker's heart. That conversion is
          the engine.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Reversal</h3>

        <p>The pattern researchers label <strong>DARVO</strong> — <em>Deny, Attack, and Reverse
        Victim and Offender</em>, Jennifer Freyd's term from the wider research on betrayal trauma
        — describes what typically happens when the concern surfaces: denial, attack on the person
        raising it, and then the reassignment of the roles, so that the leader is now the injured
        party under attack and the complainant is the aggressor. Numbers 16 supplies the vocabulary
        for step three, which is why it is reached for. <strong>The Korah charge is the reversal
        move in its religious form.</strong></p>

        <p>Wade Mullen's work on <em>impression management</em> documents the same sequence at
        institutional scale — the concealment, the minimising language, the pre-emptive narrative,
        the apology carefully constructed to concede nothing. Diane Langberg's
        <em>Redeeming Power</em> supplies the theological frame: power is a gift held in trust, and
        the deformation is always the same — power used for the self, then protected by more power.
        Chuck DeGroat traces the personality dimension; Michael Kruger writes as a seminary
        president to the specific problem of the bullying pastor whose ministry is otherwise
        fruitful, which is the case institutions find hardest.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Achan Move</h3>

        <p>There is a specific, recognisable manoeuvre worth naming because Scripture names it.
        When an institution is in trouble, the trouble is attributed to the person who disclosed
        it: <em>you are the one harming the church.</em> Ahab to Elijah: <strong>"Is it you, you
        troubler of Israel?"</strong> — and Elijah's answer is the model reply: "I have not
        troubled Israel, but you have"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Kings 18:17-18">1 Kings 18:17–18</a>).</p>

        <p><em>The scandal is not the disclosure. The scandal is the thing disclosed.</em></p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="crime">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">When It Is a Crime</h2>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Four Corrections</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Matthew 18 is not a confidentiality policy for crimes.</p>
          <p>It governs "if your brother sins against you" — a dispute between brothers, aimed at
          gaining the brother. It does not create a private jurisdiction that displaces the civil
          magistrate, and it was never read that way until it became convenient.</p>
          <p><strong>Romans 13 is the relevant text, and it points the other way.</strong> The
          civil authority "is God's servant for your good… he does not bear the sword in vain. For
          he is the servant of God, an avenger who carries out God's wrath on the wrongdoer"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 13:4">Romans 13:4</a>). Reporting a crime to the police
          is not a failure of church process. It is obedience to the institution God appointed for
          exactly this — by the same author, in the same letter, that the church has spent five
          hundred years citing for submission.</p>
          <p><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 6">1 Corinthians 6</a> does not forbid
          it.</strong> Paul addresses believers taking one another to court over "trivial cases"
          (6:2) — property and civil disputes among brothers. He is not legislating about crimes,
          and the same author invokes his legal rights against magistrates in
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 16:37">Acts 16:37</a> and appeals to Caesar in
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 25:11">Acts 25:11</a>.</p>
          <p><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 5:19">1 Timothy 5:19</a> does not
          shield.</strong> "Do not admit a charge against an elder except on the evidence of two or
          three witnesses" is a rule of evidence, protecting elders from casual accusation — and it
          is immediately followed by 5:20: "As for those who persist in sin, rebuke them in the
          presence of all," and 5:21: do this "without prejudging, doing nothing from partiality."
          The passage that requires care before believing a charge requires <strong>public</strong>
          rebuke once it is established, and forbids favouritism in both directions. It is
          routinely quoted at verse 19 and stopped there.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Mandatory Reporting</span>
          Where mandatory reporting law applies — and in most jurisdictions clergy are covered,
          though the details vary and should be checked locally rather than assumed — the question
          does not arise at all. The obligation is legal, and a church that treats compliance as a
          spiritual failure has placed itself against Romans 13.
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="otherway">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">When the Charge Runs the Other Way</h2>

        <p>A treatment of spiritual abuse that could not be used by a falsely accused leader would
        be a bad treatment. This section is not a concession; it follows from the same principles.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Four Counterweights</span>
          <p><strong>False accusation is a named, serious sin.</strong> The ninth commandment.
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 19:16-21">Deuteronomy 19:16–21</a> provides that a
          witness who testifies falsely receives the penalty he sought for the accused — the most
          severe deterrent in the judicial code.
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Proverbs 6:19">Proverbs 6:19</a>: "a false witness who breathes
          out lies, and one who sows discord among brothers."</p>
          <p><strong>Mobs are real, and they are not made righteous by being online.</strong> The
          dynamics that produce a pile-on — moral certainty, distance, no cost, no cross-examination
          — are the same dynamics Numbers 16:41 describes when "the whole congregation" turns on
          Moses and Aaron the day after the judgment.</p>
          <p><strong>Some critics are Korah.</strong> The passage exists. Ambition genuinely does
          cloak itself in the language of equality — Josephus and <em>m. Avot</em> converge on this,
          and both were describing something they had watched happen. A subordinate leader who
          builds a faction, works the congregation privately, and aims at the senior position while
          speaking the language of reform is doing precisely what Numbers 16 describes, and calling
          it by its name is not abuse.</p>
          <p><strong>Grievance is not evidence.</strong> Sincerity is not evidence. Suffering is not
          evidence. The tests above apply to the accuser as strictly as to the accused, and a leader
          is entitled to a fair process, a presumption of innocence, the identity of the charge
          against him, and an adjudicator who is not the complainant.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Symmetric Principle, Which Is the Whole of It</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">No one should be the judge in their own cause. This is what
          Moses does right in Numbers 16, and it protects both parties.</p>
          <p>A leader must not adjudicate accusations against himself. A complainant must not be the
          sole adjudicator of whether the response was adequate. <strong>The answer to both failures
          is the same: an actual process, with people in it who have nothing to gain.</strong>
          Independent investigation is not a concession to the world's methods. It is the
          institutional form of "in the morning the LORD will show."</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="wounded">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Wounded — What the Canon Says to Them</h2>

        <p>Most treatments of this subject are addressed to institutions. This one is addressed to
        the person, because the person is who is left afterward, and because Scripture speaks to
        them at length and in a register the literature cannot reach.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Lament Is Authorised Speech</h3>

        <p>Roughly a third of the Psalter is complaint. It is not a lapse tolerated by a patient
        God; it is <strong>the form of prayer he canonised.</strong> A tradition that treats a
        wounded person's protest as a spiritual problem has lost a third of its own prayer book.</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          It is not an enemy who taunts me — then I could bear it… but it is you, a man my equal,
          my companion, my familiar friend. We used to take sweet counsel together; within God's
          house we walked in the throng.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 55:12-14">Psalm 55:12–14</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Betrayal by someone inside the sanctuary, named as its own category of injury, in
        Scripture, without rebuke. <em>The reason church hurt is not like other hurt is that God
        already said so.</em></p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Korahite Psalter</h3>

        <p>Here the study's own material closes a circle. The sons of Korah did not die
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 26:11">Numbers 26:11</a>). They became gatekeepers and
        singers. Eleven psalms carry their name — and the psalms they carry are,
        disproportionately, the psalms of people who have lost the assembly.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">The Psalms of People Who Lost the Assembly</span>
          <p><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 42:3-4">Psalm 42:3–4</a></strong> — "My tears
          have been my food day and night, while they say to me all the day long, 'Where is your
          God?' These things I remember, as I pour out my soul: how I would go with the throng and
          lead them in procession to the house of God with glad shouts and songs of praise."
          <em>Grief at being cut off from the congregation, remembering when he led it.</em></p>
          <p><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 43:1">Psalm 43:1</a></strong> — "Vindicate me,
          O God, and defend my cause against an ungodly people; from the deceitful and unjust man
          deliver me!" <em>A prayer for vindication that has not arrived yet.</em></p>
          <p><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 88">Psalm 88</a></strong> (Heman the Ezrahite,
          a Korahite) — the darkest psalm in the Psalter, which does not resolve. "You have put me
          in the depths of the pit… darkness is my closest friend." <strong>The canon contains a
          prayer with no upturn in it.</strong> No one is required to finish the sentence with a
          resolution they do not have.</p>
          <p><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 84:10">Psalm 84:10</a></strong> — "I would
          rather be a doorkeeper in the house of my God than dwell in the tents of the wicked."
          Korah's sin was refusing to be a doorkeeper. Numbers 16:26 — "depart from the tents of
          these wicked men." <em>His sons became doorkeepers, and sang the sentence back as
          praise.</em></p>
          <p><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 49:15">Psalm 49:15</a></strong> — "But God will
          ransom my soul from the power of Sheol, for he will receive me." <em>Numbers 16:33 —
          "they went down alive into Sheol."</em></p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Family Destroyed by the Misuse of This Story Got the Microphone</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Scripture took the vocabulary of the judgment — the pit,
          Sheol, the earth giving way, the tents of the wicked — and put it in the mouths of the
          judged man's line, as worship.</p>
          <p>If Numbers 16 has been used against you, the Psalter's answer is not an argument. It is
          a hymnbook, written by the people it was used against, and it is still in the canon.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">God's Own Posture</h3>

        <ul>
          <li><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Ezekiel 34:11-16">Ezekiel 34:11–16</a>.</strong>
          After ten verses against the shepherds: "Behold, I, I myself will search for my sheep and
          will seek them out… I will rescue them from all places where they have been scattered on
          a day of clouds and thick darkness… I will seek the lost, and I will bring back the
          strayed, and I will bind up the injured." God does not delegate this. The chapter that
          indicts the shepherds ends with God taking the job himself — and then gives it to David,
          which the New Testament reads as Christ
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 10:11">John 10:11</a>).</li>
          <li><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="John 9:35">John 9:35</a>.</strong> "Jesus heard that
          they had cast him out, and having found him he said…" <em>The expelled man does not have
          to find his way back. He is sought.</em></li>
          <li><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Isaiah 42:3">Isaiah 42:3</a>.</strong> "A bruised
          reed he will not break, and a faintly burning wick he will not quench."</li>
          <li><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 34:18">Psalm 34:18</a>.</strong> "The LORD is
          near to the brokenhearted and saves the crushed in spirit."</li>
          <li><strong><a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 4:15">Hebrews 4:15</a>.</strong> The high
          priest who is not unable to sympathise — and who was himself tried by a religious court,
          condemned by the sanctuary establishment, and executed on the recommendation of the high
          priest.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="leaving">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Leaving, Staying, and Whether It Is Schism</h2>

        <p>The question people actually ask. There is no formula, but there are relevant data.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Five Considerations</span>
          <p><strong>Leaving is not automatically schism.</strong> Numbers 16:26 — "Depart, I pray
          you, from the tents of these wicked men" — is in the same chapter as everything else, and
          it is God's instruction to <em>separate from</em> those under judgment. The text used to
          keep people in place also contains the command to move. Paul tells Timothy to "avoid such
          people" (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 3:5">2 Timothy 3:5</a>), in the letter that
          quotes Numbers 16:5.</p>
          <p><strong>Nor is it automatically righteous.</strong> Part III set out the seriousness of
          division at length, and the temptation to leave every church that disappoints is real and
          is its own failure. The question is not <em>am I unhappy</em> but <em>is the thing that is
          wrong here one the body can address, and is it willing to.</em></p>
          <p><strong>Safety is not a matter for deliberation.</strong> Where there is danger to a
          person — particularly a child — there is no discernment question about remaining, and
          there is no church process that outranks getting out.</p>
          <p><strong>Practical markers that the situation is not correctable:</strong> concerns have
          been raised through the actual channels and produced only a response about the person
          raising them; the investigation is controlled by the accused; the pattern is longstanding
          and known; those who left before were characterised rather than heard. Where these hold,
          the question has generally already been answered by the institution.</p>
          <p><strong>Do not leave alone.</strong> Anxiety about being on the wrong side of a church
          division responds poorly to more private argument at two in the morning. The tradition's
          counsel on scrupulosity, in every communion, is uniform: this is not resolved by further
          solitary investigation. Talk to someone outside the system — a pastor elsewhere, a
          counsellor, a friend who knew you before.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Either Way</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">If you stay, or if you go, the thing that was done to you
          did not change your standing with God.</p>
          <p><em>The Lord knows those who are his</em>
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 2:19">2 Timothy 2:19</a>) — Numbers 16:5, quoted by
          an apostle, in a divided assembly, to say that the sorting belongs to God and not to the
          people doing the sorting. That verse was written for exactly this.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="repentance">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Repentance and Restoration</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">What Repentance Looks Like</h3>

        <p><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Corinthians 7:10-11">2 Corinthians 7:10–11</a> distinguishes
        godly grief from worldly grief and then lists the marks of the real thing:
        <em>earnestness, eagerness to clear yourselves, indignation, alarm, longing, zeal,
        punishment.</em> Note that the list is behavioural and public. Add:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Restitution.</strong> Zacchaeus: "if I have defrauded anyone of anything, I
          restore it fourfold" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 19:8">Luke 19:8</a>) — unprompted,
          specific, costly, and quantified.</li>
          <li><strong>Fruit.</strong> "Bear fruits in keeping with repentance"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 3:8">Luke 3:8</a>) — over time, and assessed by others.</li>
          <li><strong>Naming the act, not the reaction.</strong> An apology for "how this was
          received," "any hurt caused," or "mistakes made" concedes nothing. The statement that
          admits a fact is the only kind that can be checked.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Forgiveness Is Not the Same as Trust, Reconciliation, or Reinstatement</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Forgiveness is commanded
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 6:14">Matthew 6:14</a>;
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 4:32">Ephesians 4:32</a>) and it is not optional. It is
          also not the same as any of the following, and collapsing them is the mechanism by which
          people are pressured back into harm.</p>
          <p><strong>Trust</strong> is earned and evidence-based.
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 10:16">Matthew 10:16</a> — <em>wise as serpents.</em></p>
          <p><strong>Reconciliation</strong> takes two. "If possible, so far as it depends on you"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 12:18">Romans 12:18</a>) explicitly contemplates that it
          may not be possible.</p>
          <p><strong>Restoration to fellowship</strong> is the church's goal for a repentant person
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 6:1">Galatians 6:1</a>;
          <a class="ref" data-ref="2 Corinthians 2:5-8">2 Corinthians 2:5–8</a>).</p>
          <p><strong>Restoration to office is a separate question with its own criteria.</strong>
          The qualifications for an overseer are <em>above reproach, sober-minded, self-controlled,
          not violent but gentle, not quarrelsome, not a lover of money,</em> and <em>well thought
          of by outsiders</em> (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 3:2-7">1 Timothy 3:2–7</a>).
          These are standing requirements, not entry requirements. <strong>A man who no longer meets
          them is not being punished by being out of office; he is simply not qualified,</strong>
          and forgiving him does not re-qualify him. Consequences are not unforgiveness.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">A Documented Case</span>
          The historical case worth knowing here is the <strong>shepherding / discipleship
          movement</strong> of the 1970s, in which a group of prominent teachers built a system of
          personal "covering" and submission that produced widespread, documented harm — and in
          which several of the leaders, notably Bob Mumford in his public 1989 statement, later
          apologised for it by name. It is a rare, checkable instance of the thing being admitted
          from the inside, and it is worth citing precisely because the admission exists.
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="afterward">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Faith Afterward</h2>

        <p>The transfer is the danger. What was done in God's name gets attributed to God, and the
        person who leaves an abusive church often finds they have also lost the ability to pray.
        Three things are worth saying, and none of them is an argument.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Three Things</span>
          <p><strong>1. The distinction is biblical, not a coping device.</strong> Ezekiel 34 exists
          because God does not identify himself with the shepherds. He says "I am against" them and
          "I myself will search." The one thing God is unambiguously not, in that chapter, is on
          their side.</p>
          <p><strong>2. "Do not neglect to meet together" is not the whip it is used as.</strong>
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 10:25">Hebrews 10:25</a> sits inside the paragraph about
          <em>drawing near</em> with full assurance and <em>stirring one another up to love and good
          works.</em> It describes a gathering that does something for you, not an attendance
          requirement enforced by the people harming you. Someone who cannot yet walk into a building
          has not violated Hebrews 10; they are the person Hebrews 10 was written to reassure.</p>
          <p><strong>3. Slow is allowed.</strong> Psalm 88 does not resolve.
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 13">Psalm 13</a> asks "how long" four times before it turns.
          The canon does not require a timetable, and anyone who gives you one is adding to
          Scripture.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Centre of the Study, Applied Here</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Access to God was never in their gift.</p>
          <p>Korah's question was <em>may I draw near?</em> The answer the New Testament gives is
          <strong>yes</strong> — by the blood of Jesus, through a curtain torn from top to bottom
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 10:19-22">Hebrews 10:19–22</a>). It is not administered
          by a leader, it cannot be revoked by an elder board, and it does not run through the person
          who hurt you. That is the whole point of the torn veil, and it is the reason the passage
          that has been used to control people is, read to the end, the passage that makes control
          impossible.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="leaders">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">For Those Who Lead</h2>

        <p>The material above is more useful to a leader than to anyone else, because it is the only
        party who can act on it before there is a case.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Seven Controls</span>
          <p><strong>1. Never be the judge in your own cause.</strong> If a complaint concerns you,
          you do not run the process, choose the investigators, define the scope, or announce the
          finding. This is the one procedural rule the passage actually yields, and Moses kept it
          under far greater provocation than you will face.</p>
          <p><strong>2. Assume you cannot see this in yourself.</strong> "Let anyone who thinks that
          he stands take heed lest he fall"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 10:12">1 Corinthians 10:12</a>). No one in the
          literature reports having recognised it unaided. The 250 were <em>men of renown.</em></p>
          <p><strong>3. Build the exits.</strong> A congregation in which a person can raise a
          concern, be heard by someone with no stake, and leave with a blessing is structurally
          resistant to this. Publish the route. Make sure it does not terminate in you.</p>
          <p><strong>4. Audit the money in public.</strong> Moses
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 16:15">Numbers 16:15</a>), Samuel
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Samuel 12:3">1 Samuel 12:3</a>), and Paul
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 20:33-35">Acts 20:33–35</a>) all volunteered their financial
          conduct for inspection at the moment their authority was in question. Three for three.</p>
          <p><strong>5. Watch your vocabulary.</strong> If your habitual response to criticism
          includes <em>Korah, Absalom, Jezebel, touch not, unteachable, bitter, divisive</em> — you
          are converting claims into diagnoses, and you should stop before you find out why.</p>
          <p><strong>6. Handle a grievance like Acts 6.</strong> A minority faction alleged unfair
          administration. The apostles did not call it rebellion, did not invoke their office, and
          did not ask whether the complainants had standing. <strong>They conceded the problem and
          restructured</strong> — and the chapter ends with the word of God increasing.</p>
          <p><strong>7. Preach the whole passage.</strong> Numbers 18:1 — the priests "shall bear the
          iniquity connected with the sanctuary." The settlement of the Korah affair ends by
          assigning liability to the office. If you preach the chapter's authority and not its
          liability, you have preached half of it, and you have kept the better half.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="yield">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">What This Part Yields</h2>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Eight Findings</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The veil is torn. Whatever was said in that room, it did not
          move the curtain back.</p>
          <p><strong>1. Numbers 16 does not address abusive authority, because the case does not
          arise in it.</strong> Moses is righteous by stipulation, and every charge against him is
          false.</p>
          <p><strong>2. The canon addresses abusive authority at length, and severely.</strong>
          Eli's sons, Isaiah 56, Jeremiah 23, Ezekiel 34, Micah 3, Zechariah 11, Matthew 23,
          2 Corinthians 11:20, 3 John, 1 Peter 5. The woes run downward at shepherds, not sideways at
          members.</p>
          <p><strong>3. The charge cannot be self-verifying.</strong> Amaziah made it against Amos in
          the correct technical vocabulary and was wrong. The decisive question is who is rendering
          the verdict, and whether they have anything to gain by it.</p>
          <p><strong>4. The tests discriminate but do not always resolve</strong> — and protective
          action does not require a verdict. The church habitually gets this backwards, rendering
          confident verdicts on hearts while taking no protective action at all.</p>
          <p><strong>5. False accusation is its own sin, and the same principle governs both.</strong>
          No one judges their own cause; both parties are entitled to a process with people in it who
          have nothing to gain.</p>
          <p><strong>6. Forgiveness is commanded; trust, reconciliation, and office are three other
          questions.</strong> A man who no longer meets 1 Timothy 3 is not being punished by being
          out of office.</p>
          <p><strong>7. The wounded have a psalter, and its authors are the sons of Korah.</strong>
          They lost the assembly, they wrote it down, they did not resolve it prematurely, and the
          canon kept all of it — including Psalm 88, which never turns.</p>
          <p><strong>8. Access is not in anyone's gift.</strong></p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-application">
          <span class="tg-application__label">For Teaching and Preaching</span>
          <p><strong>1. Say out loud, from the pulpit, that this passage has been misused.</strong>
          Congregations in which the misuse has never been named cannot tell the difference between
          the text and the way they have heard it used. Naming it costs a sentence and changes how
          everything after it is heard.</p>
          <p><strong>2. Preach Numbers 16 with Ezekiel 34 in the same series.</strong> The chapter
          about rebelling against a good shepherd and the chapter about shepherds who feed themselves
          belong together, and neither is safe alone.</p>
          <p><strong>3. Read Numbers to 18:1.</strong> The Korah affair is settled by loading
          liability onto the priesthood. That is the ending, and it is almost never preached.</p>
          <p><strong>4. Use Acts 6 as the counter-case.</strong> A grievance against leadership,
          conceded and remedied, with growth as the result.</p>
          <p><strong>5. Teach Numbers 11:29 first.</strong> "Would that all the LORD's people were
          prophets." Moses wanted what Korah wanted.</p>
          <p><strong>6. End on the Korahite psalms.</strong> The family that this story destroyed
          sang Psalm 84 and Psalm 88, and both are Scripture. Any treatment of Korah that stops at
          the fire has stopped reading too early — and the people most likely to notice are the ones
          the passage has been used against.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div class="tg-reading">
        <h2 class="tg-reading__title">For Further Study</h2>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Subtle Power of Spiritual Abuse</cite>, David Johnson and Jeff VanVonderen.
          Bethany House, 1991. <em>The book that named the category for most of the English-speaking
          church; still the clearest description of the system from inside.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Redeeming Power</cite>, Diane Langberg. Brazos, 2020.
          <em>The best theological treatment of power as a trust and of what its deformation does to
          both parties. Langberg is a clinical psychologist of forty years' standing in this
          field.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Something's Not Right</cite>, Wade Mullen. Tyndale, 2020.
          <em>On impression management — how institutions and individuals manage disclosure. The
          chapter on non-apologies is worth the book.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Bully Pulpit</cite>, Michael Kruger. Zondervan, 2022.
          <em>Written by a seminary president to the specific problem of the bullying pastor whose
          ministry is otherwise fruitful — the case institutions find hardest.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>When Narcissism Comes to Church</cite>, Chuck DeGroat. IVP, 2020.
          <em>On the personality dimension and on what happens to a congregational system around
          it.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Escaping the Maze of Spiritual Abuse</cite>, Lisa Oakley and Justin Humphreys. SPCK,
          2019. <em>The empirical UK research behind the safeguarding definition; useful for readers
          who want the contested category argued rather than assumed.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Blind to Betrayal</cite>, Jennifer Freyd and Pamela Birrell. Wiley, 2013.
          <em>The research background for institutional betrayal and the DARVO pattern.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Dark Clouds, Deep Mercy</cite>, Mark Vroegop. Crossway, 2019.
          <em>On lament as authorised speech — the practical companion to the Korahite psalms
          section above.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Shepherds After My Own Heart</cite>, Timothy Laniak. IVP, 2006.
          <em>The full biblical theology of shepherding, including the judgment texts; the necessary
          background to Ezekiel 34.</em>
        </p>
      </div>
